hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1211","From Malik, from Nafi; he said: The slave-girls of Abdullah ibn Umar would wash his feet while they were menstruating, and hand him the khumrah."
"1212","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim; he said: My mother sent to Alqamah: May the menstruating woman nurse the sick? He said: Yes; when you attend her, then leave from her presence. He said: I said: Will you wash me when I die? He said: No."
"1213","From Abdullah; he said: Ibn Umar's feet would be washed by the menstruating woman, and he would pray over the menstruating woman [obscure wording in source]."
"1214","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Thabit ibn Ubayd, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Hand me the khumrah. She said: I am menstruating. He said: It is not in your hand."
"1215","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, that Abu Zabyan sent to Ibrahim asking him about the menstruating woman performing wudu for me, then my leaning upon her and praying; he said: No."
"1216","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Masruq; he said: I entered upon Aishah and said: O Mother of the Believers, what is permitted for a man of his menstruating wife? She said: What is short of the private part. He said: So Masruq nudged with his hand a man who was with him, as if to say: Listen. He said: I said: Then what is permitted for me of her while fasting? She said: Everything except intercourse. Mamar said: It has reached me that a wife of Ibn Umar would hand him the khumrah while menstruating."
"1217","From Mamar, from Khusayf, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas; he said: If he has intercourse with her while menstruating, he gives a dinar in charity."
"1218","From Ibn Jurayj, from Khusayf, from Miqsam, the freedman of Abdullah ibn al-Harith, who reported to him, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having had intercourse with his wife while menstruating, and he ordered him to give half a dinar in charity. Ibn Jurayj said: And al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Miqsam, would say: I do not know whether Miqsam said a dinar or said half a dinar."
"1219","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf and Ali ibn Bazimah, from Miqsam: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a man who approached his wife while menstruating to give half a dinar in charity."
"1220","Mamar ibn Rashid and Ibn Jurayj reported; they said: Abd al-Karim reported to us, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas; he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever approaches his wife during her menstruation, let him give a dinar in charity, and whoever approaches her when the blood has turned away from her but she has not bathed, then half a dinar. All of that is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Mamar ibn Rashid reported to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1221","Ibn Jurayj reported; he said: Abd al-Karim reported to us, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set for the menstruating woman a portion of a dinar, when he approaches her before she bathes."
"1222","Hisham reported from al-Hasan that he would analogize him to the one who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan. Hisham said: And Ibn Sirin said: Nothing is upon him; he seeks Allah's forgiveness. And Mamar said it from al-Hasan."
"1223","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, and from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim; they said: Nothing is upon him; he seeks Allah's forgiveness."
"1224","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata; he said: I asked him about the menstruating woman whose husband has intercourse with her; he said: I have not heard of a prescribed expiation for it, so let him seek Allah's forgiveness."
"1225","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that a man said to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq: I saw in a dream that I was urinating blood. He said: You are a man who approaches his wife while she is menstruating, so seek Allah's forgiveness and do not return to it."
"1226","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported; he said: I heard Makhul being asked about the man who approaches his wife while menstruating; he said: He seeks Allah's forgiveness and repents to Him."
"1227","From Umar ibn Habib, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: And do not approach them until they are pure, then when they purify themselves, approach them from where Allah commanded you - he said: Women have two purities: the purity of His saying until they are pure, meaning when they become free of blood before bathing, and His saying when they purify themselves, meaning when they bathe; and she is not lawful for her husband until she bathes. He says: Approach them from where Allah commanded you means from where the blood exits; and if he does not approach her from where he was commanded, he is not of the repentant nor of the purified."
"1228","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: A man asked Ata: The menstruating woman sees purity but does not bathe - is she lawful for her husband? He said: No, until she bathes."
"1229","From Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, that Salim ibn Abdullah and Sulayman ibn Yasar were asked about the menstruating woman, whether her husband may approach her when she sees purity before bathing; they said: No, until she bathes."
"1230","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata; he said: I said to him: Does the menstruating woman make up the prayer? He said: No, that is an innovation."
"1231","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah; he said: He was asked: Does the menstruating woman make up the prayer? He said: No, that is an innovation."
"1232","From Mamar, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Muadhah al-Adawiyyah; she said: I asked Aishah: Why does the menstruating woman make up the fasts but not make up the prayers? She said: Are you a Haruri? I said: I am not a Haruri, but I am asking. She said: That used to happen to us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we were ordered to make up the fasts and were not ordered to make up the prayers. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Muadhah, from Aishah, the like of it."
"1233","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, from Aishah; she said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not order any woman among us to make up the prayer."
"1234","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri; he said: The menstruating woman makes up the fasts. I said: From whom? He said: This is what the people have agreed upon, and not in everything do we find an isnad."
"1235","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, and Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father; they said: When the menstruating woman becomes pure before the night, she prays Zuhr and Asr, and when she becomes pure before dawn, she prays Maghrib and Isha. From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, and from Layth, and from Tawus, the like of it; from Israil, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, the like of it."
"1236","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata; he said: If she becomes pure at the beginning of the day, let her complete her fast; otherwise, no."
"1237","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: It was reported to me from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf; he said: When the woman becomes pure before sunset, she prays all the daytime prayers, and when she becomes pure before dawn, she prays all the night prayers."
"1238","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan; he said: When she becomes pure at the time of Asr, she prays Asr and does not pray Zuhr."
"1239","From Mamar, from Qatadah; he said: When the menstruating woman becomes pure at the time of a prayer, she prays that prayer, and when she does not become pure in its time, she does not pray that prayer."
"1240","From Mamar, from Qatadah; he said: When the woman sees purity at the time of a prayer but does not bathe until its time passes, let her repeat that prayer, making it up. And al-Thawri said it."
"1241","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shubrumah, from al-Shabi; he said: When the woman menstruates at the time of a prayer she had not prayed, she makes it up when she becomes pure."
"1242","Mamar reported; he said: Qatadah was asked about a woman who slept through the last Isha, then woke up menstruating; he said: When she becomes pure, let her make it up."
"1243","From al-Thawri, from Yaqub ibn Ata, from his father; he said: Concerning the menstruating woman who sees purity at night but does not bathe until morning; he said: She bathes, completes her fast, and there is no make-up upon her."
"1244","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: The woman wakes up menstruating, then becomes pure in part of the day - does she complete it? He said: No, she is one who makes up. From Mamar, from Qatadah, the like of the saying of Ata."
"1245","From Mamar, from a man who heard Ikrimah saying: When she menstruates before sunset in Ramadan, she eats and drinks."
"1246","From Mamar, from Hammad and Qatadah; they said: When she menstruates after Asr while fasting, she breaks her fast and makes up."
"1247","From Mamar, and Qatadah; he said: When she menstruates before the night, there is no fast for her, and when she wakes up menstruating then becomes pure in part of the day, there is no fast for her."
"1248","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: A woman wakes up menstruating and sees nothing until she becomes pure; he said: She replaces it. I said: And a woman who menstruates at the end of the day - does she complete what remains? He said: No, she has menstruated, so she must replace it."
"1249","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that a man has intercourse with his wife and she does not bathe until she menstruates; he said: She performs ghusl. And he said: Menstruation is weightier than janabah; the junub may pass through the mosque, but the menstruating woman may not pass."
"1250","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, and I asked him about it; he said: Menstruation is greater."
"1251","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, and from al-Thawri, from Hisham, from al-Hasan; he said: She performs ghusl."
"1252","From Mamar, from Qatadah; he said: She washes her private part, then that suffices her."
"1253","From Mamar; he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the menstruating woman and the junub, whether they remember Allah; he said: Yes. I said: Do they recite the Quran? He said: No."
"1254","Mamar said: And al-Hasan and Qatadah would say: They do not recite anything of the Quran."
"1255","Ibn Jurayj reported; he said: I said to Ata: What do the menstruating woman and the junub recite of the Quran? He said: As for the menstruating woman, she recites nothing, and as for the junub, (only) the verse... [source breaks off here]."
"1256","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: Do the menstruating woman and the junub remember Allah? He said: Yes."
"1257","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim; he said: The menstruating woman and the junub remember Allah and mention His name."
"1258","From al-Thawri, from Amir al-Shabi; he said: I heard Abu al-Gharif al-Hamdani saying: I witnessed Ali ibn Abi Talib urinate, then say: Recite the Quran as long as none of you is junub; but when he is junub, then not even a single letter. And Abd al-Razzaq holds this view."
"1259","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abidah al-Salmani; he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab disliked reciting the Quran while junub."
"1260","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Tariq; he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyab: Does the junub recite anything of the Quran? He said: Yes."
"1261","From Hisham ibn Hassan; he said: The junub glorifies Allah, praises Him, supplicates, but does not recite a single verse."
"1262","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: What does one without wudu recite? He said: Four or five verses."
"1263","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus; he said: One without wudu recites verses, and he would not name their number. He said: And Ibn Jurayj said it from Ibn Tawus."
"1264","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: A man wants to review the Quran, reciting outside prayer - should he perform wudu like the wudu of prayer, thoroughly with wiping the head? He said: Yes."
"1265","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri; he said: Ibn Abbas would permit one without wudu to recite more than a verse or two."
"1266","From Malik, from Nafi; he said: Ibn Umar would not recite the Quran except in purity. From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying the like of the saying of Ibn Umar."
"1267","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Said ibn Jubayr; he said: I heard Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas saying: We recite our portions of the Quran after hadath without touching water."
"1268","Mamar reported from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, saying: I would sometimes hear Abu Hurayrah reciting, running through the surah quickly, while he was without wudu."
"1269","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin; he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab came out of the lavatory and recited a verse or verses. Abu Maryam al-Hanafi said to him: You came out of the lavatory and you are reciting? Umar said to him: Did Musaylimah give you this ruling? - and he had been with Musaylimah."
"1270","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani; he said: Ibn Masud would correct a man while he was reciting, then he stood up and urinated, and the man stopped reciting, so Ibn Masud said to him: Recite."
"1271","From Mamar, from Abu Iyas Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, that Abu Musa al-Ashari would recite without wudu."
"1272","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali; he said: Recite the Quran in every state, as long as you are not junub."
"1273","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad; he said: Aishah said: I recite my portion - or she said: my hizb - while lying on the bed."
"1274","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim; he said: Recite the Quran in every state as long as you are not junub, and enter the mosque in every state unless you are junub."
"1275","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq; he said: I heard Alqamah ibn Qays saying: He said: We entered upon Salman, and he recited verses of the Quran to us while he was without wudu."
"1276","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah; he said: We came to Salman al-Farisi, and he came out to us from a lavatory of his, so we said to him: If only you would perform wudu, O Abu Abd Allah, then recite to us such-and-such surah. He said: Allah only said: In a hidden book, none touch it but the purified - and it is the dhikr that is in heaven, which none touch but the angels. Then he recited to us of the Quran whatever we wished."
"1277","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Zurzur; he said: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah being asked about a man who recites and passes wind; he said: Let him stop reciting until the wind leaves him."
"1278","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: I relieved myself in one of these ravines; should I wipe myself with dust, then recite? He said: Yes."
"1279","From Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father; he said: In the letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Amr ibn Hazm: None touches the Quran except in purity. From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, the like of it."
"1280","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: There is no harm in one without wudu taking the mushaf and carrying it up from one place to another."
"1281","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that a man said to Ibn Abbas: I place the mushaf on a bed, have intercourse upon it, have a wet dream in it, and sweat upon it? He said: Yes (there is no harm)."
"1282","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: Do the junub and the menstruating woman touch the mushaf while it is in its case? He said: No. I said: With a cloth between their hands and its case? He said: No; the case is no more a barrier than the cloth. I said: And one without wudu while it is in its case? He said: Yes, it does not harm him. I said: May he take it closed? He said: Yes."
"1283","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata; he said: The mushaf is not touched directly by one without wudu. I said: With a cloth between their hands and its case? He said: And is the case any more a barrier than the cloth? I said: One without wudu while it is in its case? He said: Yes, it does not harm him. I said: May he take it closed? He said: Yes."
"1284","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, Tawus, and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad; they disliked touching the mushaf while without wudu."
"1285","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata; he said: I like that coins and dinars not be touched except with wudu, but people cannot avoid touching them; they are disposed to that. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ata disliked the menstruating woman and the junub touching dinars and dirhams."
"1286","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri; he said: Coins containing the Quran are not touched except with wudu."
"1287","And Mamar said: And al-Hasan and Qatadah saw no harm in it, saying: They are disposed to that."
"1288","From Hisham ibn Hassan; he said: Ibn Sirin sent me to ask al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about coins containing the remembrance of Allah, whether people trade with them while they bear writing; and I asked him, and he said: There is no harm in the writing they trade with; they only trade for the gold and silver; if you took the writing on a rag, they would give you nothing for it. But coins containing the remembrance of Allah are not touched except with wudu."
"1289","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim; he said: Coins containing the remembrance of Allah are not touched except with wudu."
"1290","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim; he said: One without wudu does not touch coins. From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the like of that, except that he said: From behind a cloth."
"1291","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he was asked about a purse containing coins taken into the lavatory; he said: People cannot do without their spending money."
"1292","From al-Thawri, from Mansur; he said: I asked Ibrahim: May I write a letter without wudu? He said: Yes."
"1293","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi; he said: It is disliked for the junub to write: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"1294","From Mamar, from Qatadah; he said: It was recommended that the hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not be recited except with wudu. From Mamar, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar; he said: Ibn al-Musayyab was asked about that and saw no harm in it."
"1295","From a shaykh of the people of Makkah; he said: I heard Sufyan al-Usfuri saying: I saw Said ibn Jubayr urinate, then wash his face, then take the mushaf and read in it. Abu Bakr said: And I heard it from Marwan ibn Muawiyah al-Fazari."
"1296","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: The Quran (as an amulet) is upon a woman and she menstruates, or janabah befalls her - should she remove it? He said: If it is in a tube, there is no harm. I said: And if it is in a rag? He said: This is more hateful to me. I said: Why do they differ? He said: The tube shields more than the rag. Ibn Jurayj said: And I heard him before that being asked: Is the Quran placed upon a boy? He said: If it is in an iron tube, or a tube of whatever kind, then yes; but a rag, no. He said: About the split tablet, which is the slate on the boy's necklace? He would say: It does not become pure [obscure wording in source]."
"1297","Mamar reported to me; he said: Alqamah ibn Abi Alqamah reported to me; he said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyab about the refuge (amulet) worn by the menstruating woman and the junub; he said: There is no harm in it if it is in a tube, or a rag covering it."
"1298","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, and from Mamar, from Mansur; they said: They would dislike hanging anything along with the Quran."
"1299","From Ibn Jurayj; he said: I said to Ata: A ring on the hand of a menstruating woman or a junub? He said: It does not harm it; there is only a letter or a small thing in the ring. I said: And one without wudu who enters the lavatory with it on his hand? He said: It does not harm it."
"1300","Ibn Jurayj and Mamar reported from Sadaqah ibn Yasar; he said: Ibn al-Musayyab was asked about a ring containing the name of Allah when janabah befalls its wearer; he said: There is no harm in it. I said: I enter the privies, and janabah befalls me; he said: There is no harm in it. And he said: Said ibn al-Musayyab gave me the ruling."
"1301","Mamar reported from Thabit, from Anas; he said: The engraving of the ring of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Muhammad [source text breaks off here]."
"1302","From Mamar, from Jabir, from Abu Jafar; he said: In the ring of Ali was: Exalted is Allah, the King."
"1303","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu Jafar; he said: In the ring of Ali was: Exalted is Allah, the King."
"1304","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the engraving of his ring was: There is no god but Allah, and he would not wear it."
"1305","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar engraved his name in his ring, and he would not wear it."
"1306","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: he disliked that a complete verse be written on a ring, except only part of it."
"1307","From Ma'mar, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil brought out to us a ring whose engraving was an image, and he informed us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore it once or twice. He said: Then one of those with us washed it and drank (the water)."
"1308","From Ma'mar, from Jabir, he said: On the ring of Ibn Mas'ud was a tree, or (something) between two flies (i.e. two protrusions)."
"1309","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: The engraving of the ring of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari was a lion between two men."
"1310","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: The engraving of the ring of Anas ibn Malik was a karki bird, or he said: a bird with two heads; and the engraving of the ring of Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah was: one-fifth belongs to Allah."
"1311","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Karim, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about a ring on which the remembrance of Allah is written, and he disliked it."
"1312","From a man, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father: al-Hasan and al-Husayn engraved the remembrance of Allah on their rings."
"1313","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra: a man said, O Messenger of Allah, may a man pray in a single garment? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you all have two garments? Ibn Jurayj added in his narration: Should I leave my clothes on the clothes-rack and pray in a single garment?"
"1314","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Umar ibn Abi Salama: he said, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, wrapped in it, having crossed its two ends over each other. Al-Thawri added in his narration: in the house of Umm Salama."
"1315","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, wrapped in it."
"1316","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The last prayer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed was in a single garment, crossing its two ends, behind Abu Bakr."
"1317","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Muhammad ibn Ali, he said: The last prayer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed was in a safflower-dyed wrapper, wrapped in it."
"1318","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from al-Husayn ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in a cloak, crossing its two ends, on a cold day, shielding himself with the cloak from the chill of the ground, like the posture of one digging with the hoof."
"1319","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: My father informed me, from Muhammad ibn Mash(?), he informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys halted to rest by some water; then Mu'adh ibn Jabal came walking and halted at that water. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke up and said: Who is this? He said: I am Mu'adh. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Mu'adh, do you have no camel? He said: No. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution, then stood and prayed, and it was as if he was adjusting his lower garment, so he wrapped it and prayed in his waist-wrapper. Then he said to Mu'adh: Get up, prepare the saddle well and make a seat for yourself. He said: I do not know how, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, saddled (the camel), made a seat for him, and mounted him behind himself."
"1320","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If the garment is wide, pray in it wrapped around you; and if it is small, pray in it as a waist-wrapper."
"1321","From Ibrahim, from al-Zuhri: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man praying in a single garment wrapped in it, and said: Do not imitate the Jews; if one of you finds only one garment, let him wrap it as a waist-wrapper."
"1322","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Qays ibn Talq: a man said, O Messenger of Allah, I sometimes pray in a single garment. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent about it until the prayer was called; then he matched his two garments together and prayed in them. Abu Bakr said: I narrated it to Ma'mar, and he said: I heard Yahya mention it."
"1323","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays in a single garment, let him cross its two ends over his shoulder."
"1324","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should pray in a single garment with nothing of it on his shoulder."
"1325","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from a man, from Abu Hurayra, that he used to say: They used to say: If the waist-wrapper is small and one cannot wrap it around oneself, let him pray in a waist-cloth."
"1326","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata say: A man may pray in a garment even if it is spacious, but let him wrap himself in it; and it is more beloved to me that he pray in a cloak along with a waist-wrapper."
"1327","Then he informed us of a report which Muhammad ibn Ali informed him of, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who was among the last of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to die. He said: We used to come to him in his house, and he led us in prayer in his house among Banu Salama, we being a group. He stood and led us, while his clothes-rack with his cloak on it was placed (nearby). He said: So he wrapped himself in a garment which, he said, did not reach his shoulders. Muhammad said: I think he said: of woven fabric. He said: I only saw him showing us that there was no harm in that."
"1328","Ibn Jurayj said: Ata said: Jabir said: I, my father, and my maternal uncle are among the Companions of al-Aqaba."
"1329","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah led us in prayer in a single garment. He said: I think he said: He wrapped it as a waist-wrapper."
"1330","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Ubayd Allah ibn Miqsam, he said: I saw Jabir ibn Abdullah praying in a single garment. He said: I said: Do you pray in a single garment while (other) clothes are beside you? He said: Yes, because of a fool like you."
"1331","From Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd, he said: Ibn al-Munkadir informed me, he said: I saw Jabir ibn Abdullah praying in a single garment, crossing its two ends."
"1332","From his father, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: Ibn Abbas led them in prayer in a single garment, crossing its two ends."
"1333","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Mas'ud ibn Hirash: Umar ibn al-Khattab led them in prayer in a single garment, wrapped in it."
"1334","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim: Khalid ibn al-Walid led us in prayer in a musaffara, wrapped in it; and al-musaffara is a sheet."
"1335","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Ibn Mas'ud differed about a man praying in a single garment. Ubayy said: He may pray in a single garment; and Ibn Mas'ud said: In two garments. That reached Umar, so he sent for them and said: You differed in a matter then dispersed, and people do not know which of the two to follow. Had you come to me you would have found knowledge with me. The correct view is what Ubayy said, though Ibn Mas'ud did his best."
"1336","From al-Hasan: Ubayy ibn Ka'b and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud differed about praying in a single garment. Ubayy said: There is no harm in it; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in a single garment, so prayer in it is permissible. And Ibn Mas'ud said: That was only when people could not find clothes; but now that they find them, prayer is in two garments. Then Umar stood on the pulpit and said: The correct view is what Ubayy said, though Ibn Mas'ud did his best."
"1337","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A man stood up to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, O Messenger of Allah, may a man pray in a single garment? He said: Do all of you find two garments?"
"1338","Then when it was the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, a man stood up to him and said: May I pray Asr in a single garment? Umar said: When Allah gives you abundance, be generous to yourselves. A man gathers his clothes upon himself: a man prays in a waist-wrapper and cloak, in a shirt and waist-wrapper, in a waist-wrapper and a qaba (long coat), in trousers and a qaba. He said: And I think he said: in drawers and a cloak, in drawers and a shirt, and in a qaba."
"1339","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is the minimum clothing in which I may pray a voluntary prayer? He said: In one garment. I said: Wrapped around? He said: Yes."
"1340","From Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr: in the letter which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Amr ibn Hazm: None of you should pray in a single garment except with its two ends crossed."
"1341","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from A'isha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in a khamisa (woolen garment) with markings; when he finished his prayer he said: Take this khamisa to Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfa and bring me an anbijaniyya (plain thick garment), for it distracted me just now from my prayer."
"1342","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar clothed him with two garments while he was a boy. He said: Then he entered the mosque and found him praying wrapped in one garment. He said: Do you not have two garments to wear? I said: Yes indeed. He said: Tell me, if I sent you behind the house, would you wear them both? He said: Yes. He said: Then is Allah more deserving that you adorn yourself for Him, or people? Nafi' said: I said: Indeed Allah."
"1343","Then he informed him from the Messenger of Allah or from Umar - Nafi' was certain it was from one of them, and I think it was only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that he said: None of you should wrap himself in prayer like the wrapping of the Jews; let him wrap himself in it: whoever has two garments, let him wear a waist-wrapper, then let him pray."
"1344","Nafi' said to me: Abdullah would not consider it right for anyone to pray without a waist-wrapper and trousers, even if it were a jubba (long coat) and cloak without waist-wrapper and trousers."
"1345","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar saw me praying in a single garment and said: Did I not clothe you with two garments? I said: Yes indeed. He said: Tell me, if I sent you to so-and-so, would you go in this garment? I said: No. He said: Allah is more deserving of the one you adorn yourself for, or the one you adorned yourself for."
"1346","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I wrap myself in the garment? He said: No; wrapping around is more covering: a man returns his waist-wrapper over his private parts twice. And he used to dislike that one wrap it as a waist-wrapper and pray in it alone when it is small. I said: Tell me, if there were two men with a waist-wrapper on each, and they have one cloak, and they stand to pray - is it more beloved to you that they wear that cloak over both of them while wearing their waist-wrappers, or that they wrap their waist-wrappers and leave the cloak? He said: Rather they both pray in their waist-wrappers, and the cloak together is more beloved to me."
"1347","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Ahwas ibn al-Hakim, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Ubada ibn al-Samit: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in a shawl or a burda (cloak), having tied it upon himself."
"1348","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mis'ar, he used to say: If a man prays in a garment doubled over the private parts, there is no harm."
"1349","From Ma'mar, he said: I saw Ibn Tawus praying in a jubba with no waist-wrapper or cloak on him, so I asked him; and he informed me that his father saw no harm in praying in a jubba alone, or a shirt alone, when it does not outline (the body)."
"1350","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: When Tawus was asked about a single garment in prayer, he said: Does every man find two garments? And he used to say: A man prays in a jubba alone, or a shirt alone when it is thick; and if the waist-wrapper is small and does not reach to wrap around with, then let him wear it as a waist-wrapper."
"1351","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray in a shirt alone? He said: Yes, when it is thick. He said: I said: (Fur) coats, may I pray in them? He said: Yes, and what is wrong with them; they have been tanned."
"1352","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: A man prays in a single shirt when it is tight (thick); there is no harm in it."
"1353","From Ibn al-Taymi, from al-Sabbah, he said: Ata and Mujahid entered upon Abd al-Hamid ibn Abdullah ibn Amr, and he asked them: A man praying in a single shirt? Ata said: Yes; and Mujahid said: It is more beloved to me that he tie something over his waist."
"1354","From Isra'il, from a man he named, and from his father: Jabir ibn Abdullah led them in prayer in a shirt with no waist-wrapper or cloak on him; and Jabir said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a shirt."
"1355","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He was asked - while I was listening - about the qaba: may a man pray in it alone? (He said:) The qaba is slit open, and were it not for that he would pray in it alone; but let him wear a waist-wrapper over it, or a waist-wrapper under it. I said to him: May a man pray in trousers alone? He said: No, unless he finds nothing else. And in the narration of Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Amr, there is clarification on that."
"1356","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray in a garment I borrowed, not knowing whether it is pure or not? He said: Yes."
"1357","From al-Thawri, he said: If a Muslim man buys a garment from a polytheist or borrows it, let him pray in it and not wash it, unless he knows of something (impure) in it."
"1358","I heard al-Thawri say: There is no harm in a man praying in the garment of a Christian, a Magian, or a Jew, unless he knows of something (impure) in it."
"1359","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to consider a sword as a cloak."
"1360","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sa'id, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: A bow is a cloak."
"1361","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Ibrahim, he said: When a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could not find a cloak to pray in, he would throw over his shoulders - or he said: over his shoulder - a camel's hobbling rope."
"1362","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw Ata letting his garment hang loose (sadl) while in prayer."
"1363","From al-Thawri, from Thawr al-Hamdani, from Ata, that he used to say: There is no harm in sadl (letting the garment hang)."
"1364","From Ma'mar, from Ibrahim: he disliked sadl."
"1365","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad al-Nakha'i: he used to let (his garment) hang loose (sadl)."
"1366","From Hisham ibn Hassan, he said: I saw al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin letting their garments hang over their shirts. From Ma'mar, from one who saw al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin doing that."
"1367","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid: he used to dislike that a man wrap his cloak around his shoulders; he said: He spreads it out."
"1368","From Abu Hanifa, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man who had let his garment hang loose while praying, so he folded his garment upon him. From al-Thawri, from a man, from Abu Atiyya al-Wadi'i, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1369","From Bishr ibn Rafi', from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Ubayda ibn Abdullah: his father disliked sadl in prayer. Abu Ubayda said: And my father used to mention that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it."
"1370","From Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: he disliked sadl in prayer. He said: And I know it only as raised (to the Prophet). From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from both of them, that they both disliked it - Mujahid, I think he said, and Tawus."
"1371","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid: he used to dislike sadl."
"1372","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: he disliked sadl, unless one holds its two ends. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I saw al-Thawri, when he prayed, gather the two ends of the garment with his hand to his chest."
"1373","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: he disliked sadl."
"1374","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'id, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: He saw a people letting their garments hang (sadilin), and said: As if they are Jews coming out of their fihr. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: What is their fihr? He said: Their synagogues."
"1375","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, he said: I heard Mujahid say: If one sees isbal (trailing the garment) while praying, let him loosen his cloak over it. I mentioned that to Tawus, and he said: That is better and finer."
"1376","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, he said: I saw Tawus praying with his cloak placed under his upper arm."
"1377","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim: he saw no harm in a man letting (his garment) hang when wearing a shirt; but if wearing a waist-wrapper, then he should not let it hang."
"1378","From Ma'mar, from a man - I think it is Amir al-Ahwal - from Ata ibn Abi Rabah: he used to dislike sadl and raised a narration about that; then he mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1379","From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: he used to pray in the garment in which a ritually impure person sweats."
"1380","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Ibn Umar was asked: May one pray in the garment in which one has intercourse? Ibn Umar said: I had intercourse last night in this garment of mine, and I pray in it."
"1381","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no harm in praying in a garment in which a ritually impure person sweats."
"1382","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: I asked A'isha about a man who contacts his wife in a garment then sweats in it. She said: When that happened, the woman would take a rag - or rags - and wipe with it, and the man would wipe with it, and they saw no harm in it - meaning: that one prays in it."
"1383","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Umm al-Hudhayl: A'isha was asked about a garment in which a menstruating woman sweats. She said: There is no harm in it - meaning: that one prays in it."
"1384","From a man of Quraysh, from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Mu'adha al-Adawiyya, from A'isha: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade praying in a woman's inner garment worn next to the skin. He said: And I heard Hisham ibn Urwa narrate, from his father, from A'isha, that she used to dislike praying in it."
"1385","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man contacts his wife, then puts on his garment, then washes his private parts, and perhaps some semen gets on his garment; then he performs the ritual bath for prayer and dries himself with that garment. He said: There is no harm in it."
"1386","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that a man said to Ibn Abbas: I place the mushaf on my Bed, have intercourse on it, have wet dreams on it, and sweat on it? He said: Yes (it is fine)."
"1387","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: There is no washing nor sprinkling required for the garment of a menstruating woman or a ritually impure person."
"1388","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, about semen getting on a garment: he said, If you do not find it filthy, remove it with an idhkhira (a fragrant grass stalk)."
"1389","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: he informed me - meaning Ata; Ata dropped from the book of Ibn al-A'rabi - that he heard Ibn Abbas say: If you have a wet dream in your garment, remove it with an idhkhira or a rag, and do not wash it if you wish, unless you find it filthy or dislike it being seen on your garment."
"1390","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, he said: A'isha sent for a guest of hers, inviting him. They said to her: He is washing (semen from) ritual impurity off his garment. She said: Why does he wash it? I used to rub it off the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1391","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Remove semen with an idhkhira (stalk) or a stone from your garment."
"1392","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Talha ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, nephew of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: If you know you had a wet dream in your garment but do not know where it is, wash the whole garment; but if you do not know whether it got on it or not, sprinkle it with water. From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the like of it. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the like of it. From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, the like of it. Al-Hasan said: But if you are certain it is in one part of the garment, wash that part and sprinkle the other part."
"1393","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib - he narrated to him that he performed Umra with Umar ibn al-Khattab in a caravan including Amr ibn al-As; and Umar halted on part of the road near water, had a wet dream, woke up when morning was near, rode on, and they hastened until they reached water. He sat at the water washing what he saw of the wet dream until dawn broke. Amr said: Morning has come, and we have clothes - wear them and leave your garment to be washed. Umar said: How strange of you, O Amr! Even if you find clothes, does every man find clothes? By Allah, had I done it, it would have become a sunna. No, rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."
"1394","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father: Umar was afflicted by ritual impurity on a journey; in the morning he said: Do you think we can reach water before sunrise? They said: Yes. So he hastened until he reached it, bathed, and began washing what he saw of the impurity on his garment. Amr ibn al-As said: Why not wear a garment other than this and pray? Umar said to him: If you find a garment, does every man find one? Had I done it, it would have become a sunna; but I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."
"1395","From Ayyub, from Nafi', from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: One who was with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, on a journey narrated to me, when he had no water with him and was afflicted by ritual impurity. He said: Do you think if we hasten we can reach water before sunrise? Umar bathed and began washing what of the impurity had got on his garment. Amr ibn al-As or al-Mughira said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, why not pray in this garment? He said: O Ibn Amr - or al-Mughira - do you want me to avoid praying in a garment touched by impurity, so that it would be said Umar did not pray in a garment touched by impurity? No, rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."
"1396","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father: he performed Umra with Umar ibn al-Khattab in a caravan including Amr ibn al-As. He halted near some water, had a wet dream, woke up in the morning, found no water in the caravan, rode on hastening until he reached water. He sat at the water washing what of the wet dream was on his garment; when dawn broke, Amr ibn al-As said to him: Morning has come - leave your garment to be washed and wear some of our clothes. He said: How strange of you, O Amr! Even if you find clothes, does every Muslim find clothes? By Allah, had I done it, it would have become a sunna. Rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."
"1397","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: If you have a wet dream in your garment and do not know the spot, sprinkle it with water."
"1398","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no (ritual) impurity on a garment."
"1399","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, about semen getting on a garment whose spot is unknown: he said, The garment is sprinkled."
"1400","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Pus, blood, urine, and madhy getting on a garment are all the same: scrape it, then sprinkle it with water."
"1401","From Ma'mar, he said: I said to al-Zuhri: A man sees a little or much blood on his garment. He said: Salim informed me that Ibn Umar used to leave (the prayer) for little or much of it, then continue from what he had prayed, unless he speaks, in which case he repeats."
"1402","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does a garment require washing? For you informed me from A'isha that she used to scrape blood until... He said: That suffices. I said: Blood, pus, everything likewise - if scraped, does that suffice? He said: Yes, scrape it, then sprinkle it, and that suffices you. I said to him: I scraped blood from my garment but could not remove it. He said: Sprinkle over it and that suffices, even if you do not wash it."
"1403","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man asked Ata, saying: On my back is skin with sores whose pus has filled my clothes, and washing burdens me. He said: Can you not put a drying powder on it to dry it? He said: No. He said: Then pray, and do not wash your garment; for Allah excuses on account of the excuse."
"1404","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: About a garment touched by blood: he said, If it is considerable, he leaves (the prayer); if little, he does not leave. He said: And he used to say: The size of a dirham is considerable."
"1405","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, he said: He saw no harm in flea blood. From Hushaym, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the like of it."
"1406","From al-Thawri, from Hurayth, from al-Sha'bi: he saw no harm in flea blood."
"1407","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he was asked about flea blood on a garment, and said: There is no harm in it."
"1408","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: he saw no harm in flea blood. From Hushaym, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artat, from Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali, the like of it."
"1409","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from a man, from Ibrahim: he was asked about flea blood on a garment, and said: Wash what you can."
"1410","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, they said: Pus is like blood."
"1411","From Nu'man ibn Abi Shayba, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: when he prayed in a garment with blood on it, he did not repeat the prayer."
"1412","From Ibn Uyayna and others, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Alqama had a cloak - or he said: a garment - with a trace of blood that had been washed but did not go away, and he used to pray in it. It was said to him: Why not put it aside and wear another? He said: Part of what has been made beloved to me is praying in it; I see in it the blood of Mi'dad. He said: We were besieging a fortress in Adharbayjan, and he was struck by a stone which split his head, and blood flowed on his face. I adjured him, so he took this cloak of mine, wrapped his head with it, and began wiping the blood, saying: By Allah, it is small, and Allah the Blessed and Exalted blesses the small. He said: And his crown was split open with a sword. He said: So Mi'dad died of that wound of his."
"1413","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, about a garment touched by blood: he said, If considerable, he leaves (the prayer); if little, he does not leave. And he used to say: The size of a dirham is considerable."
"1414","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: If there is a dirham-sized spot on your garment, repeat the prayer."
"1415","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: Ata said to me: I have prayed many times in this garment of mine with blood in it, forgetting to wash it."
"1416","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I saw al-Qasim ibn Muhammad take off his shirt because of blood; then I forgot to wash it - he saw (blood) in it. Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan used to leave (the prayer) when he saw blood on his garment."
"1417","From al-Thawri, from Hurayth, he said: Al-Sha'bi was asked about bat droppings in the mosque, and he saw no harm in it."
"1418","From Ibn Uyayna, from Isra'il ibn Musa, he said: I was with Ibn Sirin when bat urine fell on him; so he sprinkled it and said: I used not to consider sprinkling as sufficient until it reached me from six of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1419","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked Hammad about chicken droppings getting on a garment, and he said: When dry, let him rub it off."
"1420","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Mujahid, he said: He was asked about rain-mud getting on a garment; he said: He prays in it, and when dry let him scrape it."
"1421","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in dung in the sandals; then one prays in them."
"1422","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked about a man who steps on moist dung; he said: If he wishes, he wipes his feet on the ground."
"1423","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He was asked about a man walking behind camels who is hit by spray of their urine; he said: He sprinkles."
"1424","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: He saw no harm in the dung of beasts. Ma'mar said: And the urine of cattle and sheep is like that of camels."
"1425","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in camel urine; some of them used to sniff from it. He said: And they saw no harm in (that of) cattle and sheep."
"1426","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in the urine of beasts, except stagnant (pools of it)."
"1427","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Whatever (animal) whose meat you eat, there is no harm in its urine. From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata, the like of it."
"1428","From al-Thawri, from Aban, from Anas, he said: There is no harm in the urine of cud-chewing (livestock)."
"1429","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Tell me, about what I eat - do you wash your garment of its dung or urine? He said: And what is that? I said: Camels. I said: And cattle, sheep, game, and birds. He said: I would not wash my garment of that, unless I find its smell foul or it is seen on my garment. I said: And horses, for its meat was eaten. He said: Perhaps I would wash my garment of its dung or urine, though there would be no harm for me if I left it. He said: Wipe it and sprinkle it."
"1430","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan al-Asadiyya, sister of Ukkasha, she said: She came with a son of hers for whom she had applied a ligature (treatment), fearing he had udhra (throat ailment). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For what do you torment your children with these ligatures? Upon you is this Indian wood - meaning costus - for in it are four cures, among them pleurisy. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took her boy and placed him in his lap, and he urinated on him; so he called for water, sprinkled it, and the boy had not yet reached eating food. Al-Zuhri said: So snuff is used for udhra, and licking (medicine) for pleurisy. Al-Zuhri said: So the sunna continued that the urine of a boy is sprinkled and the urine of a girl is washed."
"1431","From Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba informed me that Umm Qays bint Mihsan was among the first emigrant women who pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: She informed me that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of hers who had not yet reached eating food, and she had applied a ligature on him for udhra. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For what do you torment your children with these ligatures? Upon you is this Indian wood - meaning costus - for in it are seven cures, among them pleurisy."
"1432","Ubayd Allah said: Umm Qays informed me that that son of hers urinated in the lap of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and poured it over his urine, and did not wash it. So the sunna continued with that: sprinkling over the urine of boys who have not eaten, and washing the urine of those of them who have eaten."
"1433","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn al-Mukhariq, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The urine of a girl is washed, and the urine of a boy is sprinkled. Sufyan said: And we say: As long as he has not tasted food."
"1434","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: The urine of a girl is washed, and the urine of a boy is sprinkled as long as he has not tasted food."
"1435","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from A'isha, she said: A boy was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he urinated on him, so he poured water over it."
"1436","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, about the urine of a boy: he said, An equal amount of water is poured over it. He said: So did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the urine of al-Husayn ibn Ali."
"1437","From Husayn ibn Mihran al-Kufi, he said: Layth ibn Abi Sulaym informed me, he said: Al-Hudayb, a freedman of Zaynab bint Jahsh, narrated to me, from Zaynab bint Jahsh, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sleeping in my house, when Husayn ibn Ali came toddling. I feared he would wake him, so I distracted him with something. She said: Then I was heedless of him, and he sat on the belly of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placed the tip of his penis in the navel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and urinated in it. She said: I was alarmed at that, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring water and pour it over it. Then he said: The urine of a boy is sprinkled, and the urine of a girl is washed."
"1438","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A boy who has not eaten food - do you wash his urine or stool off your garment? He said: No; I sprinkle over it, or pour over it? I said: A boy licks before eating food - ghee and honey, and that is his food. He said: Sprinkle, or pour."
"1439","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab intended to forbid hibara (Yemeni striped garments) because of urine-dye. A man said to him: Have you not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wear it? Umar said: Yes indeed. The man said: Did Allah not say: You have in the Messenger of Allah an excellent example - so Umar left it."
"1440","From Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umar intended to forbid hibara garments because of urine-dye. Then he said: We were forbidden from excessiveness."
"1441","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: Umar said: Would that we were forbidden this asb (Yemeni cloth), for it is dyed with urine. Ubayy ibn Ka'b said: By Allah, that is not for you? He said: What? He said: We wore it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Quran was being revealed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in it. Umar said: You spoke the truth."
"1442","From Ma'mar, he said: I saw al-Zuhri wearing what was dyed with urine."
"1443","From Ayyub, from Nafi': Ibn Umar used to have suits made for the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a suit costing seven hundred to a thousand dirhams."
"1444","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi': Ibn Umar - or Umar - used to forbid dyeing with urine. He said: And Umar used to have suits woven for the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); a suit reached a thousand dirhams or more than that."
"1445","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: he used to forbid dyeing with urine, and he used to have (suits) woven for the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); a suit of them reached a thousand dirhams or more than that."
"1446","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from his father, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in his sandals."
"1447","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man pray in sandals? He said: Yes, that has reached me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he prayed in them, and what is wrong with them - and in leather socks too."
"1448","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Hurayra, he said: By the Lord of this Structure (Ka'ba), I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enter the mosque with his sandals on his feet, praying likewise, then leave the mosque likewise without taking them off."
"1449","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from a man, he said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying shod and barefoot, and I saw him turn (after prayer) to his right and his left."
"1450","From Ibn al-Taymi, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Awbar narrated to me that he heard Abu Hurayra - and a man said to him: O Abu Hurayra, did you forbid people from fasting on Friday? He said: No, by my life; it was not I who forbade people from fasting on Friday - only, by the Lord of this Sanctuary - he said it three times - I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None of you should single out Friday for fasting unless they fast other days (along with it)."
"1451","He said: I did not leave him until another man came to him and said: O Abu Hurayrah! Did you forbid the people from praying in their sandals? He said: No; by Allah, the Ever-Living! I did not forbid the people from praying in their sandals, except that I, by the Lord of this Sanctuary - and he said it three times - indeed saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, here by the Maqam praying while wearing his sandals, then he left with them still on him."
"1452","From al-Thawri, from al-Suddi, he said: One who heard Amr ibn Hurayth informed me; he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying in a pair of patched sandals."
"1453","Ibn Yazid said: Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar narrated to me, from one of their elders, he said: I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying in his sandals, and he pointed toward the Maqam."
"1454","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud: that Abu Musa led them in prayer and took off his sandals, so Abdullah said to him: Why did you take off your sandals? Are you in the sacred valley?"
"1455","From Hushaym, he said: Abu Hamzah, the freedman of Banu Asad, informed me; he said: I saw Ibn Abbas praying in his sandals."
"1456","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, that he used to pray in his sandals."
"1457","From al-Thawri, from a man of al-Nakha: that Ibrahim, when the iqamah for prayer was called, would put on his sandals and pray in them."
"1458","From Dawud ibn Ibrahim, he said: I saw Wahb ibn Munabbih praying in his sandals."
"1459","From Muqatil, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying barefoot and wearing sandals."
"1460","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed with the people and took off his sandals, so the people took off their sandals; when he finished he said: What is the matter with you? They said: We saw you take them off so we took ours off, so he said: Whoever wishes, let him pray in his sandals, and whoever wishes, let him take them off."
"1461","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It was reported to me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed in his sandals, then took them off and placed them to his left; when he finished he said: Why did you take off your sandals? They said: We saw you take off your sandals so we took off ours. He said: I only took them off because Jibril came to me and said: There is filth in them; so when you come to the doors of the mosque, check them, and if there is filth on them then scrape it off, then enter and pray in your sandals."
"1462","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Talhah, from Ata: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Check your sandals at the doors of the mosque."
"1463","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from a man who narrated to him, from Abu Said al-Khudri: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was praying one day, took off his sandals, so the people took off their sandals; when he finished he said: What is the matter with you that you took off your sandals? They said: We saw you take yours off so we took ours off. He said: Jibril came to me and informed me that there was dirt on them; so when one of you comes to the mosque let him look at his sandals, and if there is dirt on them let him rub them on the ground. From Mamar, from Qatadah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of that."
"1464","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, or another, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Saib said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed on the day of the Conquest and took off his sandals, placing them to his left."
"1465","From Abdullah ibn Ziyad ibn Saman, he said: Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When one of you prays in his sandals and wants to take them off, let him take them off between his feet, and not place them to his side harming anyone with them."
"1466","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me that Ibn Munabbih said to him: Why do you place your sandals to your left, harming your companion with them? His father heard that and said: Yes indeed, place them between your feet. So Ibn Tawus would never place them except between his feet."
"1467","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was reported to me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to dislike that any part of his feet show from his sandals."
"1468","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he used to inspect his sandals when he came to the door of the mosque, whether there was any dirt on them."
"1469","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: We said to Ata: Should one pray in the padded archery glove a man shoots with while it is on him, and the rings? He said: He should remove them. We said: That involves hardship in tying the padded glove. He said: Even so; it is only the obligatory prayer, and if he prays in them there is no harm, but I prefer that he not do so. He said: I said to him: What is the padded glove? He said: It is the wide sleeve cuff. We said: And the rings? He said: The finger guards worn on the fingers when shooting."
"1470","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray while spun yarn is in my waist-wrap? He said: Yes, it is only like your garment. I said: And likewise, a stick, and scrolls containing true writings? He said: Yes, but I prefer that he place it on the ground."
"1471","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray with gold or silver in my waist-wrap? He said: No, place them on the ground, even if in a pouch. I said: It is scattered loose in my waist-wrap. He said: Pour it onto your sandals. I said: What is special about gold and silver apart from the rest? He said: Because they have a dignity that the rest do not have."
"1472","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray wearing ornamented swords? He said: I dislike it in Makkah, but elsewhere I do not dislike praying in them. I said: Even when there is no fear? He said: Yes."
"1473","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray on al-Safa while I could find a gravelly plain near me if I wished? He said: No. I said: Would stony ground without gravel suffice me instead of the gravelly plain, ground with dust in clods, while I could find a gravelly plain near me if I wished? He said: If there is dust, that suffices you."
"1474","From Mamar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw Suhayb prostrating as if warding off the dust, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Soil your face, O Suhayb."
"1475","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray in my house in a plastered prayer-place, or on marble with no dust or gravel in it? He said: I approve of that; gravel is dearer to me. I said: What if there were only where I place my face a handful of gravel, would that suffice me? He said: Yes, if it is the size of his face, or his nose and forehead. I said: Even if there is no gravel under his hands? He said: Yes. I said: Then I prefer making the whole prostration place gravel? He said: Yes."
"1476","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi, the freedman of Ibn Umar: Did Ibn Umar dislike praying on bare hard ground, seeking out gravel and dust? He said: He did not mind."
"1477","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: What if I prayed on bare hard ground - should I clear the dust away from my face? He said: Yes."
"1478","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I set apart a spot in my house, with no prayer-place in it, in which I know no harm, and pray there? He said: Yes. I said: And I need not sprinkle it? He said: No, unless you fear there is something harmful in it; then sprinkle it if you wish."
"1479","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Take a mosque in your house, for Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani said: Do not make your houses graves, and take mosques in them."
"1480","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: It was narrated to me from Anas ibn Malik: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Honor your houses with some of your prayers, and do not make them graves."
"1481","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What about a man praying on a small mat, and a cushion? He said: There is no harm in that, as long as it is not under his face and hands; even if under his knees, because he prostrates on his bare face."
"1482","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: I said to Ata: What about a man praying with a cap on in the cold, prostrating on his cap without bringing out his hands? He said: It does not harm him. I said: And without cold? He said: I prefer that he level it with the ground; if he does not, there is no harm. I said: Do you prefer that he pray on nothing but the ground, leaving all that? He said: Yes."
"1483","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to pray on a small mat over the mat of his house, outside a mosque, prostrating on it and standing on it."
"1484","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about prostrating on a carpet. He said: There is no harm in that; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray on a small mat."
"1485","From Malik, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed on a mat."
"1486","From al-Thawri, from Tawbah, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Abdullah ibn Amir, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab praying on an abqari. I said: What is al-abqari? He said: I do not know."
"1487","From Mamar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, he said: Ibn Abbas prayed on a carpet or rug that covered his house. From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it. From his father, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"1488","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Ibn Abbas prayed on a carpet that covered the house."
"1489","My father informed me, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jundah, from Said ibn Jubayr: that Ibn Abbas led them in prayer in a single garment, crossing its ends, on a carpet that had covered the house."
"1490","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in praying on a carpet or a small mat."
"1491","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Umar would have a menstruating woman wash his feet, and he would pray on a small mat. From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"1492","From al-Thawri, from Abu Wail: that Ibn Masud prayed on a haircloth."
"1493","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: I saw my father have a rug spread for him and he prayed on it, and I thought that was because the place was dirty."
"1494","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Jafar ibn Umar, or another: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was in a house and it closed in on him, so he pulled a leather mat and prayed on it."
"1495","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, he said: It reached me that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq used to prostrate, or pray, on the bare earth, lying open to it."
"1496","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: Ibn Masud would not prostrate - or he said: would not pray - except on the bare earth."
"1497","Al-Thawri said, and Muhill informed me, from Ibrahim: that he would stand on the reed mat and prostrate on the bare earth. We said: What is al-bardi? He said: The mat?"
"1498","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from one who heard Ibn Sharayh ibn Hani, from his father, narrating from Aishah, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, shielding his face with anything, meaning in prostration."
"1499","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Shabi: that Umar said: If the crowding is severe on Friday, let one of you prostrate on his brother's back."
"1500","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Musayyib ibn Rafi: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever is hurt by the heat on Friday, let him spread his garment and prostrate on it; and whoever is crowded by the people on Friday so he cannot prostrate on the ground, let him prostrate on a man's back."
"1501","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar said: When the heat hurts one of you on Friday, let him prostrate on his garment."
"1502","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When the heat hurts one of you on Friday, let him prostrate on his garment."
"1503","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala, from Mujahid, he said: When there is crowding, let him prostrate on a man. Sufyan said: And if he cannot prostrate on a man, let him wait until the people stand, then prostrate and follow them."
"1504","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the heat hurts me I do not mind prostrating on my garment; but as for prostrating on a person, no."
"1505","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, he said: When crowding is severe, nod with your head along with the imam, then prostrate on your brother. And Ibn Jurayj said it from Tawus."
"1506","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in prostrating on the folds of the turban."
"1507","Abdullah ibn Muharrar informed us, he said: Yazid ibn al-Asamm informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to prostrate on the fold of his turban. Ibn Muharrar said: And Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, from Makhul, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of that."
"1508","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I saw Makhul prostrating on his turban, so I said: Why do you prostrate on it? He said: I shield my forehead from the cold."
"1509","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: We found the people prostrating on their turbans, with one of them prostrating with his hands in his shirt."
"1510","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha: that Shurayh used to prostrate on his hooded cloak, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid used to prostrate on his turban."
"1511","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr, from Ibrahim, that he asked him: May one prostrate on the fold of the turban? He said: Prostrating on my forehead is dearer to me."
"1512","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I got a wound on my face and bound it, then I asked Ubaydah al-Salmani whether I may prostrate on it. He said: Remove the bandage."
"1513","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar used to dislike prostrating on the fold of his turban until he uncovered it."
"1514","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to pray in their woolen cloaks, hooded cloaks, and shawls, without bringing their hands out of them. We said to him: What is al-mistaqah? He said: It is a robe made by the people of Sham with two long sleeves, its opening on the chest; they wear it and tie its sleeves when they wear it."
"1515","Bishr ibn Rafi informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Ubaydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade uncovering a covering, or holding back hair, or renewing ablution. He said: I said to Yahya: What is his saying, or renewing ablution? He said: When he steps on filth while in ablution. And his saying, not uncovering a covering: not uncovering the garment from his hands when he prostrates."
"1516","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Ibn Masud, he said: We would not uncover a garment. He said: And Ibn Umar would bring out his hands, while al-Hasan would not do so."
"1517","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and Husayn, or one of them, from Ibn Abi Hazim, from a freedwoman of his called Azzah, she said: Abu Bakr addressed us and forbade us - or forbade - praying on saddlecloths."
"1518","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was reported to me that Ali used to forbid praying on the roadway."
"1519","From Mamar, from a man of the Jazirah: that Ibn Umar used to dislike relieving oneself on the road, or praying on it."
"1520","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Aidh al-Asadi, he said: I was with Ibrahim and he led me in Fajr, placing me to his right and moving away from the road. Sulayman said: It was deemed good that a man step aside to the right of the road, or pray to the right of the road."
"1521","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! Which mosque was placed on earth first? He said: The Sacred Mosque. I said: Then which? He said: Then al-Masjid al-Aqsa. He said: I said: How long between them? He said: Forty years. He said: Then wherever prayer overtakes you, pray, for it is a mosque. He said: So my father would hold the mushaf on the road, recite the prostration verse, and prostrate as he was on the road."
"1522","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike praying in the midst of graves, or in a mosque toward a grave? He said: Yes, it was forbidden. He said: What if there is a grave with room between me and it, not far, or a cubit or more to a mosque? He said: It is disliked to pray in the midst of graves."
"1523","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Do not pray with a grave between you and the qiblah; if there is a screen of a cubit between you and it, then pray."
"1524","From Mamar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw me praying by a grave and kept saying: The grave! He said: I thought he was saying: The moon! He said: So I kept raising my head to the sky looking, then he said: I am only saying: The grave - do not pray toward it. Thabit said: So Anas ibn Malik would take my hand when he wanted to pray and move away from the graves."
"1525","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Yahya, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: All the earth is a mosque, except the grave and the bathhouse."
"1526","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, they said: They used to dislike taking three houses as a qiblah: the grave, the bathhouse, and the privy."
"1527","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Do not pray toward a privy, nor a bathhouse, nor in the graveyard."
"1528","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Zabyan, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Do not pray toward a privy, nor in the bathhouse, nor in the graveyard."
"1529","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq and al-Harith, from Ali - and I think Mamar raised it - he said: Among the worst of people are those who take graves as mosques."
"1530","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Allah, do not make my grave an idol prayed toward, for Allah's anger intensified against a people who took the graves of their prophets as mosques."
"1531","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah informed me that Ibn Abbas informed him: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when death came to him, kept casting the edge of his cloak over his face, and when it distressed him he uncovered it from his face, saying: Allah's curse upon the Jews and Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as mosques. He said: Aishah says: He was warning against what they did."
"1532","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: Ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: May Allah destroy the Jews; they took the graves of their prophets as mosques."
"1533","From Nafi ibn Jubayr, that he said: It is forbidden to pray in the midst of graves, or bathhouses, and the open graveyard."
"1534","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, and he was asked about prayer in the midst of graves? He said: It was mentioned to me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The Children of Israil took the graves of their prophets as mosques, so Allah, the Exalted, cursed them."
"1535","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: I only know that he used to dislike prayer in the midst of graves with severe dislike."
"1536","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi: Did Ibn Umar dislike praying in the midst of graves? He said: We prayed over Aishah and Umm Salamah in the middle of al-Baqi. He said: And the imam on the day we prayed over Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was Abu Hurayrah, and Abdullah ibn Umar attended that."
"1537","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May one pray in the camel's resting place? He said: Yes. I said: Is it disliked to pray in the camel's kneeling place because a man urinates toward the kneeling camel, and were it not for that it would be like its resting place? He said: Then refrain from it; but if you sense nothing of that, it is like its resting place."
"1538","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, they said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Pray in the sheepfolds, and do not pray in the camel's kneeling places."
"1539","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked: May we pray in the camel's kneeling places? He said: No. He said: May we pray in the sheepfolds? He said: Yes. He said: Should we perform ablution from sheep meat? He said: No. From Mamar, from al-Amash, from a man, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"1540","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu Sabrah: that Umar ibn al-Khattab ate camel meat then prayed without performing ablution."
"1541","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from a man of Quraysh: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Pray in the sheepfolds, and wipe their nasal discharge, for they are among the beasts of Paradise. He said, meaning: sheep among them. We said: What is their nasal discharge? He said: What is in their nostrils."
"1542","From a Medinan elder called Abdullah ibn Said ibn Abi Hind, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Abi Halhalah al-Dili informed me, from Humayd ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: Treat your sheep well, wipe the discharge from them, and pray in their vicinity - or he said: in their folds - for they are among the beasts of Paradise."
"1543","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Hayyan, he said: I heard a man in Madinah saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Pray in the sheepfolds, and wipe their nasal discharge, for they are among the beasts of Paradise."
"1544","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn Mughaffal, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: When prayer overtakes you in the sheepfolds, pray; and when it overtakes you in the camel's kneeling places, withdraw, for they are of the creation of Satan - or he said: of the beholding of Satan."
"1545","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I pray in the sheep's resting place? He said: Yes. I said: Do you dislike it because of dog urine among them? He said: Then do not pray in it."
"1546","From Layth, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Make up what you can of your prayer, and what is most guarded against for it is the dogs' resting places."
"1547","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May one pray in the cattle pen? He said: Yes. He said: What if I pray in the pen like that - do I prostrate on the dung or clear a place for my face? He said: Rather, clear a place for your face."
"1548","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from his father, he said: Abu Musa al-Ashari led us in prayer in the post house on a spot with dung on it."
"1549","From Numan ibn Abi Shaybah, from Ibn Tawus and Salamah ibn Bahram: that they were with Tawus on a journey and wanted to alight in a place, but he saw traces of a dog and disliked alighting there, and went on - or he said: turned aside from it."
"1550","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas: that he used to dislike praying in a church with images in it."
"1551","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ata ibn Dinar: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do not learn the jargon of the non-Arabs, and do not enter upon them in their churches on their feast day, for wrath descends upon them."
"1552","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Aslam, the freedman of Umar, he said: When Umar came to Sham, a prominent Christian man prepared food for him and invited him, so Umar said: We do not enter your churches because of the images in them, meaning statues."
"1553","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Aslam: that when Umar came to Sham, a Christian man prepared food for him and said to Umar: I would love for you and your companions to come to me and honor me - he being a prominent Christian man - so Umar said: We do not enter your churches because of the images in them, meaning statues."
"1554","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim: that Salman al-Farisi was seeking a place to pray in, so a non-Arab woman said to him: Seek a pure heart, and pray wherever you wish. He said: You have understood well."
"1555","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Masud: that he permitted one in major impurity to pass through the mosque crossing it, and I only know he said: nor one in major impurity except passers through."
"1556","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: One in major impurity may pass through the mosque. I said to Amr: From where do you take that? He said: From the saying: nor in major impurity except passers through - travelers who find no water. And Mujahid said that as well."
"1557","From Mamar, from Mujahid, the like of it, concerning His saying: nor in major impurity except passers through: he said: travelers who find no water."
"1558","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May one in major impurity pass through the mosque? He said: Yes."
"1559","From Mamar, from Hawshab, he said: I heard Ata saying: One in major impurity does not enter the mosque, unless forced to that."
"1560","From al-Thawri, he said: One in major impurity does not pass through the mosque, unless he finds no alternative; he performs tayammum and passes through it."
"1561","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Recite the Quran in every state as long as you are not in major impurity, and enter the mosque in every state as long as you are not in major impurity."
"1562","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: A group from Thaqif came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and prayer was established, so it was said: O Prophet of Allah! These are polytheists. He said: Nothing makes the earth impure."
"1563","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me that when the polytheists of Quraysh came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in Madinah concerning their captives taken at Badr, they spent the night in the mosque of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; among them was Jubayr ibn Mutim, and Jubayr would hear the recitation of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while Jubayr was then a polytheist."
"1564","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, lodged the delegation of Thaqif in the mosque, and built tents for them in it so they could see the people praying and hear the Quran."
"1565","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Sharik, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Muhill, he said: We passed with Ali by the sunken land at Babylon, and he disliked praying in it until he had passed beyond it."
"1566","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: that Ibn Umar said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by al-Hijr, he said: Do not enter the dwellings of those who wronged themselves unless you are weeping, lest what befell them befall you. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, covered his head and hastened until he had crossed the valley."
"1567","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, he said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by al-Hijr, he said to us: Do not enter upon these punished ones unless you are weeping; if you are not weeping then do not enter upon them, lest what befell them befall you."
"1568","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What about a dog passing through the mosque - should its trace be sprinkled?"
"1569","I heard al-Thawri saying: Concerning a dog passing through the mosque, it is sprinkled."
"1570","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a menstruating woman pass through the mosque? He said: No. I said: May she enter her house prayer-place? He said: No, let her keep away from it. I said: If she entered, should it be sprinkled with water? He said: No."
"1571","From al-Thawri, he said: It is disliked for a woman while menstruating to enter her prayer-place, but she may place whatever she wishes in it."
"1572","From Malik, from Nafi, he said: The slave girls of Abdullah ibn Umar would lay the small mat for him in the mosque while they were menstruating."
"1573","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If not in ablution, may one pass through the mosque? He said: It does not harm him."
"1574","From al-Thawri, from Layth, he said: It is disliked to enter."
"1575","From Abu Hubayrah, from Abu al-Darda: that he would urinate, then enter the mosque."
"1576","From Mamar, from Qatadah: that Ibn Sirin would sit at the edge of the mosque when he came out of the lavatory with his feet on the ground, then perform ablution."
"1577","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I saw Ibn Sirin come out of the lavatory and sit on the mosque wall with his legs out, while performing ablution."
"1578","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin used to dislike a man who urinated sitting in the mosque while not pure; rather he should pass through and not sit. He said: And Jabir ibn Zayd saw no harm in sitting in it without ablution."
"1579","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Enter the mosque in every state as long as you are not in major impurity."
"1580","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: A man goes out and urinates, then comes to Zamzam and performs ablution. He said: There is no harm in that; and if he needs to relieve himself, let him enter; if he wishes let him perform ablution at Zamzam - the religion is easy, smooth. A man said to him: I see people performing ablution in the mosque. He said: Sit; there is no harm in that. I said: Do you perform ablution in it? He said: Yes. I said: Do you rinse the mouth and nose? He said: Yes, and I complete my ablution in the mosque of Makkah."
"1581","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm performing ablution in the mosque of Makkah, and Tawus used to perform ablution in the Sacred Mosque."
"1582","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that Ibn Umar used to perform ablution in the mosque."
"1583","From al-Thawri, concerning ablution in the mosque, he said: If there is no urine, there is no harm in it."
"1584","From al-Thawri, he said: And Abu Harun al-Abdi informed me that he saw Ibn Umar performing ablution in the mosque."
"1585","My father informed me, he said: I saw Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani performing ablution in the great mosque of Sanaa."
"1586","From Ibn Abi Rawwad, he said: I saw Tawus performing ablution in the mosque. Abu Bakr said: And I saw Ibn Jurayj performing ablution in the Sacred Mosque, sitting on a carpet of his, rinsing his mouth and nose."
"1587","From Mamar, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me that his father used to perform ablution in the mosque."
"1588","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: In the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when a man saw a dream he would narrate it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: So I wished for a dream to narrate to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: And I was an unmarried youth, and I used to sleep in the mosque in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. So I saw in sleep as if two angels took me and went with me to the Fire; it was folded like the folding of a well, and the Fire had something like the two horns of a well - meaning the two horns of the well: the two wooden posts of the well - and in it were people I knew. So I began saying: I seek refuge with Allah from the Fire. Then another angel met them and said: You will not be frightened. So I narrated it to Hafsah, and Hafsah narrated it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: What a good man Abdullah is, if only he would pray at night. Salim said: So afterward Abdullah would sleep at night only a little."
"1589","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar saw no harm in sleeping in the mosque. He said: He used to sleep in it."
"1590","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in sleeping in the mosque."
"1591","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, he said: Al-Mughirah ibn Hakim al-Sanani narrated to us, he said: My father sent me to Said ibn al-Musayyib asking him about sleeping in the mosque, and he said: Where did the people of al-Suffah sleep? And he saw no harm in it."
"1592","From Mamar, from Qatadah, Mamar said: And a man narrated to him, from Alqamah al-Muzani, he said: The people of al-Suffah used to spend the night in the mosque. Alqamah said: A man among them died and they opened his waist-wrap and found two dinars in it, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Two brands."
"1593","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike spending the night in the mosque? He said: Rather, I love it, loving slumber in it."
"1594","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata would sleep in the mosque for thirty years, then stand for tawaf and prayer."
"1595","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Haytham, he said: Mujahid forbade me from sleeping in the mosque."
"1596","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Khulayd Abu Ishaq, who said: I asked Ibn Abbas about sleeping in the mosque, and he said: ""If you sleep (in it) for the sake of prayer and tawaf, then there is no harm."""
"1597","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, who said: I heard Abu Amr al-Shaybani saying: Abdullah ibn Masud used to patrol the mosque by night and would not leave any dark (sleeping) figure but turned him out, except a man who was praying."
"1598","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Haram ibn Uthman, from the two sons of Jabir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we were lying down in his mosque, and he struck us with a palm-leaf stalk that was in his hand, and said: ""Get up; do not sleep in the mosque."""
"1599","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from a man of the people of al-Suffah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invited me, along with a group of the people of al-Suffah with me, and we had supper with him, then he said: ""If you wish, you may sleep here, and if you wish, in the mosque."" So we said: ""In the mosque?"" He said: So we used to sleep in the mosque."
"1600","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: (What about) a man who breaks his ablution in the mosque of Makkah, or in his prayer-place in the house, deliberately, not while sleeping? He said: ""It is more beloved to me that he not do it."" I said: But if he did it, is any sprinkling (of water required)? He said: ""No."""
"1601","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, that a Bedouin urinated in the mosque, so the people turned on him and rebuked him harshly, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Leave him, and pour over his urine a bucketful of water or a bucket of water, for you were sent as makers of ease, and you were not sent as makers of hardship."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer with the Bedouin behind him, and while they were praying, the Bedouin said: O Allah, have mercy on me and Muhammad, and have mercy on no one else along with us. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said to him: ""You have confined what is vast."""
"1602","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, who said: A Bedouin urinated in the mosque and they wanted to beat him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Dig up its place, and throw a bucket of water over it. Teach, and make easy, and do not make difficult."""
"1603","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Yahya ibn Said, that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the mosque, a Bedouin entered and urinated in a corner of the mosque, so the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shouted at him and wanted to make him get up, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them, until when he had finished, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered, and water was poured over his urine, or it was said, a bucketful of water. Then he said: ""This is a place in which one does not urinate; it was built only for prayer."""
"1604","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Sharik ibn Abi Namr, from Ata ibn Yasar, that a Bedouin urinated in the mosque, so the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose against him, and he said: ""Do not interrupt him."" When he had finished, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a bucketful of water, and it was poured over his urine. He said: Ibrahim said: And Kathir ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, the like of it."
"1605","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: A Bedouin urinated in the mosque and the people set upon him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Dig up its place, and throw a bucket of water over it. Teach, and make easy, and do not make difficult."""
"1606","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Harun ibn Abi Aishah informed me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque, he would say: ""Peace be upon the Prophet and the mercy of Allah; O Allah, open for me the doors of Your mercy and Paradise."" And when he went out, he would say: ""Peace be upon the Prophet and the mercy of Allah; O Allah, protect me from Satan and from all evil."""
"1607","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abdullah ibn al-Hasan, from Fatimah bint Husayn, from Fatimah al-Kubra, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque, he would say: ""O Allah, bless Muhammad; O Allah, forgive me my sins and open for me the doors of Your mercy."" And when he went out, he would say the like of it, except that he would say: ""The doors of Your bounty."""
"1608","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, and he mentioned the like of it, except that he says: From Abd al-Malik ibn Said, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you enter the mosque, say: O Allah, open for us the doors of Your mercy, and when you go out, say: O Allah, we ask You of Your bounty."""
"1609","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque, he would say: ""In the name of Allah; O Allah, open for me the doors of Your mercy and make easy for me the doors of Your provision."""
"1610","Mamar informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Ibn Abbas, when he entered the mosque, would say: ""Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah."""
"1611","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, who said: When you enter the mosque, greet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when you enter upon your family, say: Peace be upon you, and when you enter a house in which there is no one, say: Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah."
"1612","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Dhi Huddan, who said: I asked Alqamah, I said: What do you say when you enter the mosque? He said: ""I say: Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, and may Allah and His angels bless Muhammad."""
"1613","From Abu Mashar al-Madani, from Said ibn Abi Said, that Kab said to Abu Hurayrah: Keep two things from me: when you enter the mosque, greet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Allah, open for me the doors of Your mercy, and when you go out, say: O Allah, bless Muhammad; O Allah, protect me from Satan. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, the like of it."
"1614","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: When you go out of the mosque, say: In the name of Allah; I place my trust in Allah; I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of what He has created."
"1615","From Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym, who said: I heard Abu Qatadah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When one of you enters the mosque, let him not sit down until he prays two rakas."""
"1616","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Nadr, who said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman said to me: What prevents your master, when he enters the mosque, from praying two rakas? For it is of the Sunnah."
"1617","From Ibn Uyaynah, from a man, from al-Shabi, who said: When you enter the mosque and bow (in prayer), then go out, then enter again, the first bowing suffices you."
"1618","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to Ata: Was it said: When a man passes by the mosque, let him bow (pray) two rakas in it? He said: ""I have not heard that, yet it is good."""
"1619","From Mamar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: A man entered the mosque, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""Do not sit down until you pray two rakas."""
"1620","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq and others of the people of Kufah, from Ibn Masud, who said: Among the signs of the Hour is that a man passes through the mosque and does not bow (pray) two rakas."
"1621","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: I saw Ibn Umar enter the mosque and leave it without praying in it."
"1622","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: When you pray, you are conversing privately with your Lord, so do not spit in front of you nor to your right, but to your left; and if there is something on your left that preoccupies you, then spit under your foot."
"1623","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw some phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque and scraped it off with a clod or with something, then said: When one of you stands for prayer, let him not clear his throat (by spitting) in front of him nor to his right, for on his right is an angel; but let him do so to his left or under his left foot."
"1624","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the mosque and saw some phlegm in the qiblah, and when he finished his prayer, he said: When one of you prays, he is conversing privately with his Lord, and Allah faces him with His face, so let none of you spit toward the qiblah nor to his right. Then he called for a stick and scraped it off with it, then called for khaluq (perfume) and smeared it."
"1625","From Mamar, from Ayyub, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) scraped it off, then sprinkled saffron over its trace, which he had called for; that is why saffron came to be used in the mosques."
"1626","From Mamar, who said: I asked al-Zuhri about saffron in the mosque, and he said: It is good; it is the perfume of the mosque."
"1627","From Ibn Abi Rawwad, from Mansur ibn Talhah al-Hajabi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the mosque and saw some phlegm in the qiblah, and when he finished his prayer, he said: When one of you prays, his Lord converses privately with him. Then he said: Who is your imam? They said: Abu so-and-so. So he removed him. Then his wife was told, so she ordered water and washed it and prepared it, and I think he said: and she fumigated the mosque. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque, he said: Who did this? They said: The wife of so-and-so. So he restored her husband as imam."
"1628","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you stands for prayer, let him not spit in front of him, for he is conversing privately with Allah so long as he is in his place of prayer, nor to his right, for on his right is an angel; but let him spit to his left or under his feet."
"1629","Mamar informed us, from Said al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from his father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying, then he cleared his throat under his foot, then rubbed it with his sandal while it was on his foot."
"1630","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Tariq ibn Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you pray, do not spit in front of you nor to your right; spit toward your left if it is empty, otherwise under your foot. And he gestured with his foot and scraped the ground."
"1631","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, who said: We were with Hudhayfah, and Shabath ibn Ribi stood up to pray and spat in front of him. When he finished, he said: O Shabath, do not spit in front of you nor to your right, for on your right is the recorder of good deeds; spit to your left and behind you. For when a man performs ablution well and stands for prayer, Allah faces him with His face, conversing privately with him, and does not turn away from him until he himself turns away or commits an evil impurity."
"1632","From Ibn Uyaynah and Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard a man of the people of Sham called Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw some phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque and scraped it off with a pebble or with something, then said: What makes this one sure that it will not be a brand between his eyes? One of the two said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for khaluq or for saffron and smeared it with it."
"1633","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Wasmi, from a man of Banu Fazarah called Ziyad ibn Milqat, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The mosque shrinks from phlegm just as a piece of meat or a piece of skin shrinks in the fire."
"1634","From Ibn al-Taymi, who said: Humayd al-Tawil informed me that he heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays, let him not spit in front of him nor to his right, but to his left; and if he does not do that, let him spit in the edge of his garment. And he said: Like this, and he folded his garment and rubbed it in it."
"1635","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: Let a man spit in prayer to his left, and if he finds no place, let him lift his left foot and spit beneath it."
"1636","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, who said: When Tawus spat in the mosque, he would dig a small hollow for it, then bury it."
"1637","From Israil, from al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi, from Asma ibn al-Hakam al-Fazari, who said: I asked a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about spittle in the mosque, and he said: It is a sin, and its expiation is to bury it."
"1638","From Mamar, from Aban, who said: A man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cleared his throat by night and brought a lamp, and buried it."
"1639","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Anas, who said: Phlegm in the mosque is a sin, and its expiation is to bury it."
"1640","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, who said: When he spat in the mosque, he would dig deep for it, then bury it."
"1641","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: We were with Abdullah ibn Masud, and he wanted to spit while there was nothing empty on his right, yet he disliked spitting to his right even though he was not in prayer."
"1642","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Nadrah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Muadh ibn Jabal, who said: He was ill and spat to his right, or wanted to spit, and said: I have not spat to my right since I became Muslim."
"1643","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Nuaym informed me that he heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz saying to his son Abd al-Malik, when he had spat to his right while on a journey: He forbade him that and said: You annoy your companion; spit to your left."
"1644","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, who said: Prescribed punishments are not carried out in the mosques. He said: And I know he only said: Nor is striking therein, meaning retaliation."
"1645","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to Ata: Was flogging in the mosque forbidden? He said: Yes."
"1646","From Mamar and Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shubrumah, who said: I saw al-Shabi flogging a Jew a prescribed punishment in the mosque."
"1647","From Israil, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, who said: I saw al-Shabi beating a man who had slandered another man in the courtyard, and he did not beat him in the mosque."
"1648","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, who said: A man was brought to Umar for something, and he said: Take him out of the mosque and beat him."
"1649","From al-Thawri, who said: I heard him, or one who heard him informed me, narrating from Jabir, from Abu al-Duha, who said: Marwan was asked about beating in the mosque, and he said: The mosque has a sanctity."
"1650","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me: We heard that it is forbidden to beat in the mosque."
"1651","One who heard Amr ibn Dinar narrating from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim informed me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade reciting poetry, requiting wounds, and carrying out prescribed punishments in the mosque."
"1652","Yahya ibn al-Ala and Muhammad ibn Muslim informed us, from Amr, from Tawus, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Prescribed punishments are not carried out in the mosques."
"1653","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Nafi, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab would say: Do not raise idle clamor, meaning in the mosque. He said: One day he entered the mosque and found two men whose voices had risen, so they rushed at him and he caught one of them and beat him, and said: Where are you from? He said: From Thaqif. He said: This mosque of ours - voices are not raised in it."
"1654","From Abd al-Quddus, who said: Nafi informed us, from Ibn Umar, who said: Umar heard a man raising his voice and said: Where are you from? He said: From Thaqif. He said: From which land? He said: From the people of Taif. He said: Had you been of the people of this town of ours, I would have hurt you with beating; voices are not raised in this mosque of ours."
"1655","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It reached me from Nafi that Umar, when he went out to prayer, would call out in the mosque: Beware of clamor; and he would say: Keep clear of (noise in) the mosque."
"1656","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: (What about) shouting in the mosque? He said: As for (ordinary) speech, there is no harm in it; but as for one who speaks obscenely or insults, no."
"1657","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Ubayy ibn Kab heard a man calling out for a lost animal in the mosque, and he spoke harshly to him. Abu al-Mundhir was told: You were not obscene. He said: We were commanded with that."
"1658","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: Hassan ibn Thabit recited poetry in the mosque, and Umar passed by him and glanced at him, so Hassan said: By Allah, I recited in it before one better than you. He feared he would cast (a mention of) the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at him, so he let him pass and left him."
"1659","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Usayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that a poet came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the mosque and said: Shall I recite to you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No. He said: Yes, permit me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go out of the mosque. So he went out of the mosque. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him a garment and said: This is in place of what you praised your Lord with."
"1660","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: It is disliked to use the tooth-stick in the mosque and to clip the nails in it."
"1661","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to Ata: May I pick my teeth in the mosque? He was startled and said: In prayer? The other said: No. He said: Yes, if he wishes."
"1662","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Tawus saying: A man announced his lost animal in the mosque, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May he not find his lost animal."
"1663","From al-Thawri, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man announcing a lost red camel of his in the mosque, saying: Who calls to the red camel? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May you not find it; mosques were built only for what they were built for."
"1664","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Musab ibn Muhammad, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O announcer, other than you will find it; mosques were not built for this."
"1665","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, and said: O announcer, other than you will find it."
"1666","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Ibn Sirin or another, who said: Ibn Masud heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, so he seized him and rebuked him, and said: We were forbidden this."
"1667","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban saying: It was said: When the announcer announces the lost animal in the mosque, say: May Allah not return it to you; and when he buys or sells in the mosque, say: May Allah not profit your trade."
"1668","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Abd Allah, from Makhul, from Muadh ibn Jabal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep your mosques away from your insane and your children, from raising your voices, drawing your swords, your buying and selling, carrying out your prescribed punishments, and your disputes; perfume them on the day of your Friday, and place your places of purification at their doors."
"1669","From Abd al-Quddus ibn Habib, who said: I heard Makhul saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep your mosques away from children and the insane."
"1670","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, that Yazid ibn al-Asamm informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep your mosques away from children and the insane. From al-Thawri, from Thawr, from two men between him and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of the hadith of Ibn Muharrar."
"1671","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: If any of the affair of the people were to me, I would not leave two men disputing in the mosque."
"1672","From Mamar, who said: Al-Hakam ibn Utaybah informed me that he saw Shurayh judging in the mosque; and I myself saw Ibn Abi Layla judging in the mosque."
"1673","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to Ata: Was drawing the sword in the mosque forbidden? He said: Yes; and it was forbidden to pass arrows through the mosque except held by their blades."
"1674","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, who said: Jabir ibn Abdullah was asked about drawing the sword in the mosque, and he said: We used to dislike that; and a man was giving arrows in charity in the mosque, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him not to pass with them through the mosque except holding all their blades."
"1675","Al-Thawri informed us, from Aslam, from Ibn Abza, who said: Drawing the sword in the mosque was disliked."
"1676","Al-Thawri informed us, from Layth, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you pass with arrows through the markets of the Muslims or their mosques, hold them by the blades, lest you wound anyone with them."
"1677","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this tree, meaning garlic, let him not come near this mosque of mine. He said: I think he means the raw (garlic) that has not been cooked."
"1678","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What you mentioned as forbidden in the mosque - is it in all mosques, or in the Sacred Mosque alone apart from them? He said: Rather, in all mosques."
"1679","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this tree, meaning garlic, let him not harm us in our mosque."
"1680","From Mamar, from Abu Harun, from Abu Said al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats this tree, meaning garlic, let him not come near this mosque of mine, nor come to us wiping his forehead. He said: I said: O Abu Said, is it unlawful? He said: No; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only disliked it because of its smell."
"1681","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis, from al-Ala ibn Abd Allah ibn Khabbab, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats this foul tree, let him not come near this mosque of ours."
"1682","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Ata ibn Yasar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats this foul tree, let him not harm us in our mosque; let him sit in his house."
"1683","Ibn Uyaynah said: I heard Abu al-Zubayr narrating from Jabir, who said: Garlic was not in our land in those days."
"1684","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said: When you plaster a mosque containing unbaked bricks with dung, do not pray in it until you wash it, if it (the dung) was free of impurity."
"1685","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, that Ubayd ibn Umayr saw a louse on Ibn Umar in the mosque, so he took it and buried it, with Ibn Umar looking at him and not objecting to that."
"1686","From Mamar: I narrated it to Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, and he said: May Allah have mercy on you, do you think every hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached Ibn Umar? Then Yahya said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sees a louse, let him not kill it in the mosque, but let him wrap it in his garment, and when he goes out, let him kill it."
"1687","From Mamar, from Abu Ghalib, that Abu Umamah saw a louse on his clothes while he was in the mosque, so he took it and buried it in the mosque, with Abu Ghalib looking at him."
"1688","From al-Thawri, from Fitr, from Shimmr ibn Atiyyah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Umamah, that he used to delouse himself in the mosque."
"1689","From al-Thawri, from Muslim, from Zadhaan, from al-Rabi ibn Khuthaym, that Ibn Masud took a louse and buried it in the mosque, then said: Did We not make the earth a receptacle, for the living and the dead (77:25-26)."
"1690","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: They saw no harm in burying a louse in the ground while one is in the mosque."
"1691","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was informed from one who saw Abu Ayyub al-Ansari killing a louse in the mosque between two pebbles."
"1692","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, the freedman of al-Tawamah, that he saw Abu Hurayrah burying a louse in the mosque and saying: Phlegm is worse than it."
"1693","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, who said: I asked Ibrahim about a man who kills a louse in prayer? He said: It is nothing."
"1694","From Thawr ibn Yazid, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Malik ibn Yukhamir, who said: I saw Muadh ibn Jabal killing lice and fleas in prayer."
"1695","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Hasan, who said: There is no ablution for killing a louse. He said: And Ibn Sirin held (the view of) ablution."
"1696","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Damdam, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to kill the two black ones in prayer: the snake and the scorpion."
"1697","From Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kill the scorpion and the snake in every state."
"1698","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: He was asked about a man who kills a scorpion in prayer? He said: Prayer is preoccupation enough."
"1699","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not contend with the two filthy ones in prayer: stool and urine."
"1700","From Hisham ibn Hassan, that he heard Ikrimah narrating from Ibn Abbas, who said: That I carry him in the side of my cloak is more beloved to me than to contend with stool and urine."
"1701","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, who said: We were with Abdullah ibn al-Arqam al-Zuhri, and prayer was established, then the need for stool came, so it was said to him: What is this? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When prayer is established and one of you wants to relieve himself of stool, let him begin with the stool."
"1702","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Arqam, who said: We were with him on a journey, and he would lead them; when the time of prayer came, he said: Let one of you lead you, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When prayer comes and one of you needs to relieve himself, let him begin with the need."
"1703","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: We went out on a pilgrimage or lesser pilgrimage with Abdullah ibn al-Arqam al-Zuhri, and he established the prayer, then said: Pray, and went off for his need. When he returned, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When prayer is established and one of you wants to relieve himself of stool, let him begin with the stool."
"1704","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Layth, from Mujahid, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do not hold back the two filthy ones in prayer: stool and urine."
"1705","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Hudhayfah, who said: I guard against one of them as I guard against the other: stool and urine."
"1706","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, who said: We tie it up tightly."
"1707","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah, from Ibrahim, who said: So long as stool and urine do not hurry you in prayer, there is no harm."
"1708","From Mamar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, that he saw no harm in that, so long as he does not fear it will distract him from his prayer by getting ahead of him."
"1709","From Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Let none of you pray while holding back urine and tawf, meaning stool."
"1710","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: Prayer was enjoined as fifty, then reduced until it was made five; then it was called out: O Muhammad, the word is not changed with Me, and for you with these five is fifty."
"1711","From Mamar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, who said: Prayer was enjoined upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night he was taken by night: prayer as fifty, then reduced until it was made five; then Allah said: For you with the five is fifty; a good deed is tenfold."
"1712","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan mentioning that it was enjoined upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night he was taken by night as fifty, then reduced to five. Al-Hasan said: And it was called out that I have effected My ordinance and lightened for My servants, and that for you with these five is fifty."
"1713","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Qurrah ibn Khalid, who said: I heard al-Hasan saying: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day - until he completed the verse. He said: The first prayer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed was Zuhr; Jibril came to him and said: And we are the ranged in ranks, and we are the glorifiers. He said: So Jibril stood before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) behind him, then the people behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the women behind the men. He said: So he prayed Zuhr with them as four, until when it was Asr time, Jibril stood and did the like. Then Jibril came when the sun had set and prayed with them three, reciting in the first two aloud and not audible in the third. He said: Al-Hasan said: And it is the witr of the daytime prayer. He said: Until when it was Isha time and the twilight had gone and night had darkened, Jibril came to him and stood before him and prayed with the people four rakas, reciting aloud in the two. Until when morning came that night, he prayed with him and the people with him like what he had done, praying with them two rakas, reciting in them and prolonging the recitation. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until he had delimited for the people their prayer. Then al-Hasan mentioned Friday, saying: So he prayed with them two rakas, dropping two for the gathering of the people that day and for the sermon. Allah said: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day and watches of the night; good deeds remove evil deeds; that is a reminder for the mindful. And he mentioned the two ends of the day: from the morning prayer to the dawn prayer, and watches of the night: Maghrib and Isha."
"1714","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Razin, who said: Nafi ibn al-Azraq disputed with Ibn al-Abbas, saying: Do you find the five prayers in the Quran? He said: Yes. Then he recited to him: So glory be to Allah when you evening and when you morning - Maghrib and Fajr; and in the afternoon - Asr; and when you noon - Zuhr. He said: And after the Isha prayer."
"1715","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Nafi ibn Jubayr and others said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke in the morning after the night in which he was taken by night, nothing startled him but Jibril descending when the sun had declined, and that is why it was called al-Ula (the First). So he ordered, and it was called out among the people: Prayer in congregation. They gathered, and Jibril prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people, prolonging the first two rakas, then shortening the remaining two. Then Jibril greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted the people. Then in Asr likewise, and they did as they had done in Zuhr. Then he descended at the first of the night, and it was called out: Prayer in congregation. So Jibril prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people, reciting in the first two, prolonging and aloud, and shortening in the remaining two. Then Jibril greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted the people."
"1716","Abu Said Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Bishr al-Ubri al-Basri informed us, saying: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Abbad al-Dabari narrated to us, saying: We read to Abd al-Razzaq ibn Hammam, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: The Muslims were concerned about something by which to gather for their prayer. Some said: A bell; and some said: A horn. Then Abdullah ibn Zayd al-Ansari was shown in a dream that a man passed by him with a bell, and Abdullah said to him: Will you sell this? The man said: What will you do with it? He said: We will strike it for our prayer. He said: Shall I not guide you to something better? He said: Yes. He said: Say: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; I bear witness there is no god but Allah, I bear witness there is no god but Allah; I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, come to prayer; come to success, come to success; Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah. He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab saw the like of that in his dream. When Abdullah prayed Fajr, he went in the morning to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to tell him, and Umar also came in the morning and found the Ansari had preceded him, and found the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered Bilal with the call to prayer."
"1717","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions consulted how to make something, when they wanted to gather for prayer, by which to gather for it, and they decided upon the bell. He said: While Umar ibn al-Khattab wanted to buy two pieces of wood for the bell, he saw in a dream: Do not make the bell; rather, call the prayer. He said: So Umar went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to tell him what he had seen, while revelation had already come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that. Nothing startled Umar but Bilal calling the prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Revelation preceded you with that, when Umar told him of that."
"1718","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar would say: The Muslims, when they came to Madinah, would gather and await the prayer, with none calling to it. They spoke about that one day: some of them said to others: Take a bell like the bell of the Christians, and some said: Rather, a horn like the horn of the Jews. Umar said: Will you not send a man to call to prayer? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Bilal, stand and call to prayer."
"1719","From Umar ibn Dharr, who said: I heard Ibrahim al-Nakhai saying: The end of the call to prayer is: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah."
"1720","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that he would say: At the end of the call of Bilal: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah."
"1721","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Uthman, their freedman, narrated to me, from his father the elder, the freedman of Abu Mahdhurah, and from Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, who said: He said: I went out among ten youths with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Hunayn, while he was the most hateful of people to us. They called to prayer, and we stood calling to prayer, mocking them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring me these youths. He said: Call to prayer. So they called to prayer, and I was the last of them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Good; this is the one whose voice I heard. Go and call for the people of Makkah, and say to Attab ibn Usayd: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to call for the people of Makkah. And he wiped over his forelock and said: Say: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; I bear witness there is no god but Allah, I bear witness there is no god but Allah, twice; I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, twice; come to prayer, come to prayer; come to success, come to success, twice; Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah. And when you call the first of Fajr, say: Prayer is better than sleep, twice; and when you make the iqamah, say it twice: Prayer has commenced, prayer has commenced. Did you hear? He said: So Abu Mahdhurah would neither cut his forelock nor part it, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had wiped over it."
"1722","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Ibn Sad al-Qaraz, in the reign of Ibn al-Zubayr, calling the first: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, I bear witness there is no god but Allah; I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, twice; come to success, twice. I said to Amr: In the iqamah, twice? He said: I do not know how they used to say the iqamah."
"1723","From Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taifi, from Abd Rabbihi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Uthman ibn Abi al-As when he appointed him over Taif: If a man comes to you wanting to call to prayer, do not prevent him. He said: And Ibn Jurayj mentioned that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of that to Uthman."
"1724","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: A caller called for Muawiyah in Makkah, so Abu Mahdhurah carried him and threw him into the well of Zamzam."
"1725","From Mamar, from Qatadah, who said: Ibn Umar passed by a caller to prayer and said: Make your call to prayer odd (single), for the call to prayer is odd."
"1726","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, who said: Ibn Umar would say: The call to prayer is three by three."
"1727","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, that Ibn Umar, when he said in the call: Come to success, would say: Come to (good) deed, then say: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah."
"1728","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Said, from Abdullah ibn Zayd, brother of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, that while he was sleeping, he saw a man with two pieces of wood. He said: So I said to him in the dream: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wants to buy these two sticks, to make them a bell to strike for prayer. He said: So he turned with his head to the owner of the two sticks and said: I will guide you to what is better than this. So he conveyed it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered him with the call to prayer. Abdullah ibn Zayd woke up. He said: And Umar saw the like of the dream of Abdullah ibn Zayd, but Abdullah ibn Zayd preceded him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him of that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Stand and call. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I am harsh-voiced. He said to him: Then teach Bilal what you saw. So he taught him, and Bilal would call to prayer."
"1729","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Murrah and Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, that they heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was concerned with the call to prayer, until he was about to order men to stand on the towers of Madinah and call to prayer, until they used the bell, or were about to use the bell. He said: Then a man of the Ansar called Abdullah ibn Zayd saw a man on the wall of the mosque, wearing two green garments, saying: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; I bear witness there is no god but Allah, I bear witness there is no god but Allah; I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, come to prayer; come to success, come to success; Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah. Then he sat awhile, then returned and said the like of it, then said: Prayer has commenced, twice - the iqamah. So he came in the morning to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him, and he said: Teach it to Bilal. Then Umar stood and said: There came to me this night what came to Abdullah, but he preceded me. He said: I heard al-Thawri, and he called to prayer for us at Mina, saying: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; I bear witness there is no god but Allah, twice; I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), twice. So he did as mentioned in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla in the call to prayer and the iqamah in full, like the hadith."
"1730","Mamar informed us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, that Bilal would repeat the call to prayer and repeat the iqamah, and that he would begin with the takbir and end with the takbir."
"1731","From al-Thawri, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Bilal, who said: His call to prayer and his iqamah were twice, twice."
"1732","From al-Thawri, from Abu Amr, from Muslim al-Batayn, who said: One who heard the caller of Ali informed me that he would make the iqamah twice, twice."
"1733","From al-Thawri, from Fitr, from Mujahid, who said: The iqamah once, once was mentioned to him, and he said: This is something the rulers have made light of; the iqamah is twice, twice."
"1734","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, who said: Bilal would repeat the call to prayer and make the iqamah odd, except his saying: Prayer has commenced, prayer has commenced."
"1735","From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, who said: Bilal was ordered to repeat the call to prayer and to make the iqamah odd."
"1736","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Umar ibn Hafs informed me that Sad called to prayer for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Quba, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You did well, my son; when you come, then call. So Sad would call at Quba, while Bilal would not call."
"1737","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he would make the iqamah for prayer on a journey, saying it twice or thrice, saying: Come to prayer, come to prayer; come to the best of deeds."
"1738","From Mamar, from al-Hasan, who said: He would say: When he calls, he says: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great, raising his voice with it. Then he says lowering his voice: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, come to success. Then he returns, raising his voice, saying: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, I bear witness there is no god but Allah; I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, come to prayer, come to prayer; come to success, come to success; Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; there is no god but Allah. He said: And Ibn Sirin would say the like of that."
"1739","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: It is a true, established sunnah that no caller should call to prayer except with ablution. He said: It is part of the prayer, and it is the opening of the prayer, so none should call except with ablution."
"1740","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin or another, who said: A man should not call to prayer except with ablution."
"1741","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: he said: They saw no harm in the mu'adhdhin giving the adhan without wudu."
"1742","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: Does the mu'adhdhin give the adhan facing the qibla? He said: Yes. But if he is in a village, he turns to his right and his left and behind him, calling the people with the call. And if he is on a journey with few people, with a leader, or there is no one among the people to call them to the adhan, let him face the qibla for his entire call."
"1743","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ata said: If he gives the adhan and is not among a congregation, he does not turn; but if he gives the adhan among a congregation, calling someone by his adhan, let him face the House until he begins, facing it until he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, then afterwards he turns and calls to the right and left if he wishes. And Abd al-Karim mentioned it from al-Nakhai."
"1744","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: he said: When the mu'adhdhin gives the adhan, he faces the qibla until, when he wants to say: Come to prayer, he turns, then he faces the qibla again when he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, there is no god but Allah."
"1745","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: he said: They used to say: facing the qibla with the takbir and the testimony. Ibrahim said: His feet stay in their place."
"1746","From al-Thawri, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfa, from his father: he said: I saw Bilal giving the adhan and turning, and I followed his mouth here and there, with his two fingers in his ears. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a red tent of his. He said: Bilal came out before him with a small spear and planted it at al-Abtah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed toward it the Zuhr and the Asr, while dog, donkey and woman passed before him, wearing a red garment, as if I am looking at the shine of his shins. Sufyan said: We think the tent was of leather and the garment striped."
"1747","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin: that the mu'adhdhin places his forefinger in his ears."
"1748","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from Talha ibn Musarrif, from Suwayd ibn Ghafla: he said: Bilal and Abu Mahdhura used to put their fingers in their ears during the adhan."
"1749","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim: he said: They used to dislike for the mu'adhdhin, once he began his adhan, to speak until he finishes, and likewise in the iqama, and to face the qibla with the takbir and the testimony. Ibrahim said: And his feet stay in their place."
"1750","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: he said: He faces the qibla in the adhan and the iqama, and does not speak in either of them."
"1751","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan: he says: The mu'adhdhin may speak in the middle of his adhan for a need that cannot be avoided."
"1752","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: May the mu'adhdhin speak in the middle of his adhan? He said: It is better for him not to speak; but if he speaks, there is no harm."
"1753","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq: he said: It is disliked for the mu'adhdhin to give the adhan sitting, and it is disliked for a boy to give the adhan until he reaches puberty."
"1754","From al-Thawri, asked about a boy who has not reached puberty: does he give the adhan for the people and establish the prayer? He said: Yes."
"1755","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: May the mu'adhdhin give the adhan other than standing? He said: No, except due to pain. I said: Due to drowsiness or laziness? He said: No. I said: May a boy who has not reached puberty give the adhan? He said: No."
"1756","From al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhuluq: he said: I saw Ibn Umar giving the adhan while riding. He said: I said to him: With his fingers placed in his ears? He said: No."
"1757","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Ziyad ibn Nuaym, from Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Sudai: he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and the Fajr prayer came, and he said: Give the adhan, O brother of Suda. So I gave the adhan while upon my mount."
"1758","From al-Thawri, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from Qabisa ibn Burmah al-Asadi, from Ibn Masud: that he said: I do not like your mu'adhdhins to be your blind ones — I think he said — nor your reciters."
"1759","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib: that Ibn Umm Maktum used to give the adhan for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while blind, and he would not give the adhan until it was said to him: Morning has come. Abd al-Razzaq said: As for Malik, he mentioned it from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, similarly."
"1760","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal gives the adhan at night, so whoever intends fasting, Bilal's adhan should not prevent him, until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the adhan. He said: And he was blind, and would not give the adhan until it was said to him: Morning has come. Then one night Bilal gave the adhan, then came to announce to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was said to him: He is asleep, so Bilal called out: Prayer is better than sleep, and it was approved in the Fajr."
"1761","From al-Thawri, from Abu Jafar, from Abu Sulayman, from Abu Mahdhura: he said: I used to give the adhan for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the Fajr prayer, and when I said in the first adhan: Come to success, I would say: Prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep."
"1762","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that he used to say: Come to success, prayer is better than sleep."
"1763","From Mamar, from a companion of his, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Bilal to recite the tathwib in the Fajr prayer and not to recite it in others."
"1764","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Bilal: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to recite the tathwib in Fajr and forbade me from reciting it in Isha."
"1765","From Israil, from Isa ibn Abi Azza, from Amir: that he used to forbid his mu'adhdhin from reciting the tathwib except in Isha and Fajr."
"1766","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: that he used to say regarding the tathwib: When he says in the adhan: Come to success, come to success, he says: Prayer is better than sleep."
"1767","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Ibn Muslim informed me that a man asked Tawus, sitting with the people: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, when was ""Prayer is better than sleep"" said? Tawus said: It was not said in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but Bilal heard it in the time of Abu Bakr, after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said by a man who was not a mu'adhdhin, so he took it from him and gave the adhan with it. Abu Bakr did not remain but a short while, then when it was Umar, he said: If only we had forbidden Bilal from this which he innovated — as if he forgot it — and people have given the adhan with it until today."
"1768","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I asked Ata: When was ""Prayer is better than sleep"" said? He said: I do not know."
"1769","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: Umar ibn Hafs informed me that Sad was the first to say ""Prayer is better than sleep"" in the caliphate of Umar, and he said: An innovation. Then he left it. And Bilal did not give the adhan for Umar."
"1770","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard Jabir ibn Samura saying: The mu'adhdhin of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wait and would not establish the prayer until, when he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had come out, he would establish the prayer when he saw him."
"1771","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I said to Ata: What has been related to you — when the mu'adhdhin gives the adhan at night and day, he waits a while after finishing the call, then calls out in his voice: Come to prayer, repeatedly? He said: I do not know, and it has not reached me."
"1772","From Ibn Uyayna, from Layth, from Mujahid: he said: I was with Ibn Umar, and he heard a man reciting the tathwib in the mosque, and he said: Take us out, away from this innovator."
"1773","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Ziyad ibn Nuaym, from Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Sudai: he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah, and he commanded me, so I gave the Fajr adhan. Then Bilal came, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Bilal, the brother of Suda has given the adhan, and whoever gives the adhan, he gives the iqama."
"1774","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrima ibn Khalid: that Umar said to Abu Mahdhura: When you give the first adhan, give it, then recite the tathwib — I will come to you."
"1775","From a man, from Hanzala: he said: Ibn Abi Sufyan narrated to me that Umar said to Abu Mahdhura: When you give the first adhan, pray two rakas, then establish the prayer, for I shall come out to you. He said: And he used to give the adhan on the terrace of Zamzam."
"1776","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami: he said: Ali said: The mu'adhdhin has more right over the adhan, and the imam has more right over the iqama. Sufyan said: Meaning, he says: The imam says to the mu'adhdhin: Wait until I make wudu or pray two rakas."
"1777","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard Jabir ibn Samura saying: The mu'adhdhin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would give the adhan, then wait and not establish the prayer until, when he saw the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had come out, he would establish the prayer when he saw him."
"1778","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayra: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam is a guarantor, and the mu'adhdhin is a trustee. O Allah, guide the imams and forgive the mu'adhdhins."
"1779","From Ibn Uyayna, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Abu Hurayra: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mu'adhdhins are trustworthy, and the imams are guarantors. May Allah guide the imams, and forgive the mu'adhdhins."
"1780","From Mamar, from Qatada: that Ibn Umar said: The imam is a guarantor, whether he advances or delays, does well or does badly. Mamar said: Not all of the hadith is from Ibn Umar."
"1781","From Ibn Jurayh: he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ali: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whenever he heard the mu'adhdhin, would say as he says; and when he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he said: And I."
"1782","From Mamar and Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Abu Said al-Khudri: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear the call, say as the mu'adhdhin says."
"1783","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Ubaydillah ibn Asim, from Abdillah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from Abdillah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whenever he heard the mu'adhdhin giving the adhan, would say: Allah is Greatest, he said: Allah is Greatest; and when he said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, he said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah; and when he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he said the like of that; and when he said: Come to prayer, he said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah, the Most High, the Most Great."
"1784","Mamar and others informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Isa ibn Talha: he said: We entered upon Muawiya, and the caller called to prayer, saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, and Muawiya said as he said; then he said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and he said the like of that also; then he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and he said the like of that. Then he said: Thus I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying."
"1785","From Mamar, from Mujammi al-Ansari: that he heard Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, when he heard the mu'adhdhin, magnify and testify as he testified, then said: Thus Muawiya narrated to us that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying what the mu'adhdhin said; and when he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, he said: And I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Then he fell silent."
"1786","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Jafar: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whenever he heard the mu'adhdhin, would say as he says."
"1787","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man: When the mu'adhdhin said: Come to prayer, come to success, he said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah, the Most High, the Most Great. Then he said: Thus we heard your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying."
"1788","From Mamar, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Muhammad ibn Ali: that he said: Whoever says as the mu'adhdhin says, and when he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, says: And I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah — those who deny Muhammad are liars — he will have reward equal to whoever belied Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1789","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: It was narrated to me that people in the past used to listen attentively to the calling of the adhan as they listened to the Quran; the mu'adhdhin would not say anything but they would say the like of it, until when he said: Come to prayer, they said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah, the Most High, the Most Great; and when he said: Come to success, they said: What Allah wills."
"1790","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Abu Amir, from Muawiya ibn Qurra — and they said: They used to dislike that a man rise to prayer when the mu'adhdhin begins his iqama."
"1791","From Ibn al-Taymi, from al-Salt, from Alqama, from his mother, from Umm Habiba: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in her house and heard the mu'adhdhin, and said as he says; then when he said: Come to prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose to prayer."
"1792","From Jafar ibn Sulayman: he said: I heard Yahya al-Bakka saying: I saw Ibn Umar walking between al-Safa and al-Marwa with people with him, when a man with a long beard came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I love you for the sake of Allah. Ibn Umar said: But I hate you for the sake of Allah. Ibn Umar's companions blamed him and spoke to him, and he said: He exaggerates in his adhan and takes a wage for it."
"1793","From Mamar, from Qatada, from al-Dahhak ibn Qays: that a man said: I love you for the sake of Allah. He said to him: But I hate you for the sake of Allah. He said: Why? He said: You exaggerate in your adhan and take a wage for the Book of Allah."
"1794","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrima ibn Khalid: that Umar came to Mecca, and Abu Mahdhura gave the adhan, and Umar said to him: Did you not fear that it would burst? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, you came, so I wanted you to hear me. Umar said: Your land, O people of Tihama, is a hot land, so delay the cooling — then delay the cooling — meaning the Zuhr prayer — then give the adhan, then recite the tathwib — I will come to you."
"1795","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah, from al-Qasim ibn Abdillah: he said: No provision is taken for the adhan."
"1796","From Mamar, from Qatada: that he disliked taking a wage for his adhan, unless he is given something without stipulation."
"1797","From al-Aslami ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Farwa: he said: The first to provide for the mu'adhdhins was Uthman."
"1798","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: The adhan is the emblem of faith."
"1799","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I heard Ata saying: The mu'adhdhins will have the longest necks of people on the Day of Resurrection."
"1800","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father: he said: The mu'adhdhins will have the longest necks of people on the Day of Resurrection, and they will not decay in their graves."
"1801","From Mamar, from Qatada, from a man, from Abu Hurayra: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mu'adhdhins will have the longest necks of people on the Day of Resurrection."
"1802","From al-Thawri, from Talha ibn Yahya, from Isa ibn Talha, from a man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he said: The people with the longest necks on the Day of Resurrection are the mu'adhdhins."
"1803","Mamar informed us, from Mansur, from Abbad ibn Unays, from Abu Hurayra: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mu'adhdhin is forgiven as far as his voice reaches, and everything moist and dry that hears him confirms him, and the witness for him is twenty-five good deeds."
"1804","From Ibn Uyayna, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Ata ibn Yasar: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah forgives the mu'adhdhin as far as his voice reaches, and everything moist and dry that hears him confirms him."
"1805","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from his father: he said: I was in the care of Abu Said al-Khudri, and he said: O my son, when you are in the deserts, raise your voice with the adhan, for I heard him — meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — saying: There is no jinn, human, stone or tree but will testify for him."
"1806","From Mamar, from Aban, from al-Hasan: he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey of his, he heard a man saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon the fitra, upon the fitra is this one. Then he said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This one is free of shirk. Then he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This one has entered Paradise. Then he said: Come to prayer, come to success, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam has appeared — or he said: faith — by the Lord of the Kaaba. You will find this one is a shepherd, or a hunter, or a man who has gone out to the desert away from his family. He said: The people hastened to inform him of what they heard, and they found him to be a man of Aslam who had gone out to the desert away from his family."
"1807","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya: he said: The mu'adhdhin who seeks reward is like one brandishing his sword in the path of Allah."
"1808","From al-Thawri, from a shaykh, from Umar: he said: Fleshes are forbidden to the Fire — then he mentioned the mu'adhdhins. Al-Thawri said: And I heard one who mentions: That the people of heaven hear nothing from the people of earth except the adhan."
"1809","From al-Thawri, from Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim: he said: Umar said: If I could bear the adhan along with the caliphate, I would give the adhan."
"1810","From Israil, from Abu Sinan, from Abdillah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl: that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Were it not that I fear it would become a sunna, I would not have left the adhan."
"1811","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Shubayl ibn Awf: he said: Umar said: Who are your mu'adhdhins today? They said: Our freedmen and our slaves. He said: That is a great deficiency on your part."
"1812","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas: he said: A boy does not lead until he reaches puberty, but let your best give the adhan for you."
"1813","From Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Abdillah ibn Salam, from Abdillah ibn Salam: he said: No people are called to prayer at night but they are secured from punishment until morning, nor by day but they are secured from punishment until evening."
"1814","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: he said: I do not like to ever lead anyone except my household, because if he falls short in the prayer, upon him is the sin of what he fell short of his prayer and their prayer, and there are things incumbent upon the imam, and he saw that he feared he could not fulfill them."
"1815","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, or another: he said: Mujahid and a man with him went out to al-Taif, and each of them disliked leading his companion in prayer, so each prayed alone until they returned."
